"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   Since each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a bijection \\(\\sigma:[n]\\to[n]\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are  \n   \\[\n   U=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Decomposition into \u201cabove\u2019\u2019 and \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 regions.**  \n   For a column \\(c\\) let \\(r_c=\\sigma^{-1}(c)\\) be the row of its uncovered square.  \n   The squares **above** the uncovered one in column \\(c\\) are \\(\\{(r,c):1\\le r<r_c\\}\\); the squares **below** are \\(\\{(r,c):r_c<r\\le n\\}\\).  \n   Denote  \n   \\[\n   A=\\{(r,c):r<r_c\\},\\qquad B=\\{(r,c):r>r_c\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The tiles must partition \\(A\\cup B\\); no tile may intersect a square of \\(U\\).\n\n3. **Lower bound for the \u201cabove\u2019\u2019 region.**  \n   After permuting the columns according to the increasing order of the numbers \\(r_c\\) (which does not change the number of tiles needed), we may assume \\(r_c=c\\) for all \\(c\\); i.e. the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal.  \n   In this situation  \n   \\[\n   A=\\{(i,j):1\\le i<j\\le n\\},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   the strict upper\u2011triangular part of the board.  \n\n   Consider the \\(n-1\\) cells\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(k,k+1)\\mid k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subset A .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Let \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) be any axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained in \\(A\\).  \n   Because every point of \\(R\\) must satisfy row\u202f\\<\u202fcolumn, we have \\(b<c\\).  \n   Consequently a rectangle that contains the cell \\((k,k+1)\\) must satisfy \\(b\\le k\\) and \\(c\\ge k+1\\).  \n   Hence such a rectangle cannot contain any cell \\((\\ell,\\ell+1)\\) with \\(\\ell>k\\).  \n   **Thus each rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\).**  \n\n   Since the cells of \\(S\\) are all distinct and must be covered, at least \\(|S|=n-1\\) rectangles are required to tile \\(A\\).  \n\n4. **Lower bound for the \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 region.**  \n   By symmetry, the set  \n   \\[\n   T=\\{(k+1,k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subset B\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   plays the same role for the lower\u2011triangular region \\(B\\).  \n   The same argument shows that each rectangle covering a cell of \\(T\\) can contain at most one such cell, so at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(B\\).\n\n5. **Global lower bound.**  \n   The two families of rectangles are disjoint (they lie in disjoint parts of the board), therefore any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n   - For each column \\(c\\) with \\(c\\ge2\\) place a **vertical** tile covering the squares \\(\\{(r,c):1\\le r<c\\}\\) (the part of column \\(c\\) above its diagonal hole).  \n   - For each column \\(c\\) with \\(c\\le n-1\\) place a **vertical** tile covering the squares \\(\\{(r,c):c<r\\le n\\}\\) (the part below the hole).\n\n   The tiles are pairwise disjoint, none of them contains a diagonal hole, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal square.  \n   Their number is  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound (6) and the explicit tiling (7) coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n--- \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The board consists of the set  \n\\[\nB=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\]\nof unit squares.  By the condition \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 there exists a bijection  \n\\[\n\\sigma:[n]\\longrightarrow[n]\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor a column \\(c\\) let  \n\\[\nr_c=\\sigma^{-1}(c) \\qquad(\\text{the row of the uncovered square in column }c).\n\\]\nThe squares of column \\(c\\) that are not uncovered split into  \n\n\\[\nA_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r<r_c\\},\\qquad \nB_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid r_c<r\\le n\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine the **above region** and **below region** of the whole board by  \n\n\\[\nA=\\bigcup_{c=1}^{n}A_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid r<r_c\\},\\qquad \nB=\\bigcup_{c=1}^{n}B_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid r>r_c\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll tiles must lie completely inside \\(A\\cup B\\); a tile may not intersect any point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to the diagonal case  \n\nPermuting the columns of the board does not affect the number of tiles needed: if a family of tiles tiles the board for a permutation \\(\\sigma\\), applying the same column permutation to every tile yields a tiling for the permuted board.  \nHence we may reorder the columns so that the numbers \\(r_c\\) appear in increasing order.  \nAfter this reordering we have  \n\n\\[\nr_c=c\\qquad\\text{for every }c\\in[n],\n\\]\n\ni.e. the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal:\n\\[\nU=\\{(c,c)\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe two regions are symmetric; we treat \\(A\\) and then deduce the same bound for \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound for the region \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the set of \\(n-1\\) cells  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(k,k+1)\\mid k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subset A. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) be a rectangle contained in \\(A\\).  \nSince every point of \\(R\\) must satisfy row\u202f\\<\u202fcolumn, we have  \n\n\\[\nb<c. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(R\\) contains the cell \\((k,k+1)\\), then \\(a\\le k\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le k+1\\le d\\).  \nFrom \\(b<c\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k\\) and \\(c\\ge k+1\\).  \nThus any rectangle that contains \\((k,k+1)\\) is forced to lie entirely in rows \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and columns \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIn particular such a rectangle cannot contain any cell \\((\\ell,\\ell+1)\\) with \\(\\ell>k\\).  \n\nHence **each rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\).**  \n\nSince the cells of \\(S\\) are pairwise distinct and must all be covered, at least \\(|S|=n-1\\) rectangles are required to tile \\(A\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A lower bound for the region \\(B\\)\n\nBy reflecting the board across the main diagonal, the same reasoning applied to  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(k+1,k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subset B\n\\]\n\nshows that each rectangle contained in \\(B\\) can contain at most one element of \\(T\\).  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to tile \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Global lower bound  \n\nThe rectangles covering \\(A\\) and those covering \\(B\\) are disjoint (the two regions are disjoint).  \nConsequently any tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nReturn to the diagonal configuration (4).  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(c\\ge2\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_c^{\\text{up}}=[1,c-1]\\times\\{c\\},\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nwhich covers exactly the squares of \\(A_c\\) (the cells above the diagonal hole in column \\(c\\)).  \n\nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_c^{\\text{down}}=[c+1,n]\\times\\{c\\},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhich covers exactly the squares of \\(B_c\\) (the cells below the diagonal hole in column \\(c\\)).  \n\nThe rectangles in (9) are pairwise disjoint, as are those in (10); a rectangle from (9) never meets a rectangle from (10) because they lie on opposite sides of the diagonal.  \nNone of the rectangles contains a diagonal cell, so the uncovered squares remain uncovered.  \nTogether the rectangles (9) and (10) cover every off\u2011diagonal square.  \n\nThe number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{from (9)}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{from (10)}}=2n-2. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (8) and the explicit tiling (11) coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
