tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers precisely \\(L_i\\).  For each row \\(i<n\\) place a horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers precisely \\(R_i\\).  The tiles are pairwise disjoint because in each row the two tiles are separated by the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\).  The total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nAll squares of \\(U\\) are covered, and each row and column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal).  Hence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nObserve that a tile cannot contain squares from both \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) for the same row \\(i\\); otherwise the rectangle would have to cross the hole \\((i,p(i))\\), which is forbidden.  Consequently every tile lies entirely inside the *left region*  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\n\\]\n\nor entirely inside the *right region*  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i.\n\\]\n\nWe treat the two regions separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The left region  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nA_L=\\{(i,p(i)-1)\\mid p(i)>1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) the cell \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\); the cells of \\(A_L\\) are all distinct because the second coordinate \\(p(i)-1\\) is distinct for distinct rows (the map \\(i\\mapsto p(i)\\) is a permutation).  \n\nIf a rectangle contained two distinct cells of \\(A_L\\), say \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\((k,p(k)-1)\\) with \\(i\\neq k\\), then the rectangle would also contain the cell \\((i,p(i))\\) (the hole in row \\(i\\)) or \\((k,p(k))\\) (the hole in row \\(k\\)), because the rectangle’s column interval would contain both \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(k)-1\\) and therefore also the intermediate column \\(p(i)\\) or \\(p(k)\\).  Hence a rectangle cannot contain two cells of \\(A_L\\).  \n\nThus each rectangle covering part of \\(L\\) can contain **at most one** element of the antichain \\(A_L\\).  Since \\(|A_L|=n-1\\) (exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) and contributes no element), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover all of \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The right region  \n\nAnalogously define  \n\n\\[\nA_R=\\{(i,p(i)+1)\\mid p(i)<n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe same argument shows that any rectangle lying inside \\(R\\) can contain at most one element of \\(A_R\\), and \\(|A_R|=n-1\\) (exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\)).  Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Total number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile belongs to either the left family or the right family, so the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of Section 1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and Section 2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Explicit example for the case “one = 1”  \n\nTaking the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,2025\\)) and the horizontal tiles described in Section 1 yields an explicit tiling with exactly \\(4048\\) tiles.  In each row \\(i\\) the left tile covers columns \\(1,\\dots ,i-1\\) (absent when \\(i=1\\)), and the right tile covers columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,2025\\) (absent when \\(i=2025\\)).  This construction meets the requirement that each row and each column has precisely one uncovered square and uses the minimal number of tiles.  "
