n two a_i. However, what if p(i)-1 = p(k)-1? That would mean p(i) = p(k), i.e., two rows have holes in same column. But p is a permutation, so p(i) distinct for each i, thus p(i)-1 distinct as well (except possibly p(i)=1 yields p(i)-1=0 not a column index, but they exclude that row). So indeed the a_i are distinct. So each rectangle can contain at most one a_i. So at least n-1 rectangles needed to cover all a_i points. But does covering all a_i guarantee covering all squares in L? The argument is that each rectangle can cover at most one a_i, so to cover all a_i we need at least n-1 rectangles. However, maybe some squares in L could be covered without covering any a_i? But we need to cover all squares of L. The lower bound uses covering of a_i as a necessary condition: any rectangle covering any squares in L must contain at least one a_i? Actually not necessarily. Consider a rectangle that lies entirely left of all a_i points; e.g., a rectangle covering columns 1 to some column less than any p(i)-1 for rows in its span. But note that for each row i, the leftmost column of L_i is column 1, and the rightmost column is p(i)-1. So any rectangle covering any squares in L_i must have its rightmost column ≤ p(i)-1. But does it need to contain the point (i, p(i)-1)? Not necessarily. For a given row i, you could have a rectangle covering columns 1..c where c < p(i)-1, thus not containing a_i. So a rectangle could avoid a_i. However, the lower bound argument uses the fact that each rectangle can contain at most one a_i, but it doesn't claim each rectangle must contain at least one a_i. So the lower bound is that we need at least as many rectangles as the size of a maximal set of points such that each rectangle can contain at most one point. The a_i set is such a set: each rectangle can contain at most one a_i, but a rectangle could contain zero a_i. So the lower bound is not necessarily n-1; you could cover all L squares using fewer rectangles that avoid a_i points. But is that possible? Let's think: Could we cover L with fewer than n-1 rectangles? For each row i, the left region L_i is a contiguous segment of columns from 1 to p(i)-1. The shape of L is like a Young diagram shape (staircase shape) defined by the permutation. The a_i points are the rightmost cells of each row's left region. The shape L is a union of rows of varying lengths. The a_i points are the \"rightmost cells\" of each row. The claim that any rectangle in L can contain at most one a_i is true. But can we cover L with fewer rectangles than the number of rows with non-zero left region? Possibly by using vertical rectangles that span multiple rows but only cover columns that are left of all those rows' a_i points. For example, suppose we have rows i and k where p(i)-1 > p(k)-1. Then the rectangle covering columns 1..p(k)-1 and rows i..k would be fully inside L for rows i..k because for row i, p(i)-1 is larger, so the rectangle's rightmost column p(k)-1 ≤ p(i)-1, so it's okay. This rectangle would avoid a_i for row i (since a_i is at column p(i)-1 > p(k)-1). It would contain a_k (since a_k is at column p(k)-1). So each rectangle can contain at most one a_i, but can cover many rows' left region except possibly the rightmost cells for rows with larger p(i). So the lower bound of n-1 rectangles might be too high. Let's test with small n to see if we can achieve fewer than 2n-2 tiles.\n\nConsider n=2. We have 2x2 grid. Need one uncovered per row and column, so uncovered squares must be (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). Let's try identity (holes on diagonal). Then left region L: row1 left of column1: none (p(1)=1 => L1 empty). Row2 left of column2: column1 only (square (2,1)). So L consists of one square (2,1). Right region R: row1 right of column1: column2 (square (1,2)). Row2 right of column2: none. So R consists of one square (1,2). So we need to cover each of those squares. Each is a single square, which is a rectangle. So total tiles = 2. 2n-2 = 2*2-2 = 2. So matches