esults, and a \"log\" field with array of entries.\n\nBut the final instruction says \"Return your verdict as JSON matching the provided schema.\" There's no explicit schema. So we need to guess a plausible one. Usually they want something like:\n\n{\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"Level1Result\": \"...\",\n    \"Level2Result\": \"...\",\n    \"Level3Result\": \"...\",\n    \"FinalVerdict\": \"...\",\n    \"Reasoning\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"log\": [\n    {\"level\":1,\"detail\":\"...\"},\n    {\"level\":2,\"detail\":\"...\"},\n    {\"level\":3,\"detail\":\"...\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nBut we can also embed everything in top-level keys.\n\nGiven the instruction: \"Your response should provide a concise summary:\n\n* Level 1 Result: ...\n* Level 2 Result: ...\n* Level 3 Result: ...\n* Final Verdict: ...\n* Reasoning: ...\n\nAll detailed mathematical analysis should be provided in the structured JSON output (not in a separate prose log).\"\n\nThus we can produce a JSON with fields \"Level1Result\", \"Level2Result\", \"Level3Result\", \"FinalVerdict\", \"Reasoning\". The \"Level3Result\" can be an array of issues.\n\nThus we will produce:\n\n{\n  \"Level1Result\": \"CORRECT (answer = 4048)\",\n  \"Level2Result\": \"VALID\",\n  \"Level3Result\": [\"Justification Gap: The lower bound argument does not explicitly state that each rectangle must contain at least one of the points a_i (or b_i), though this follows from the definition; the omission is a minor presentation gap.\"],\n  \"FinalVerdict\": \"PASS\",\n  \"Reasoning\": \"The solution correctly identifies the uncovered squares as a permutation matrix, proves a lower bound of 2n‑2 tiles using a valid counting argument, and provides an explicit construction achieving this bound. The methods used are valid; only minor justification gaps are present, which do not affect correctness.\"\n}\n\nThat should satisfy.\n\nBut we need to ensure we quote the answer. The answer is 4048. So we can quote \"4048\". So \"answer = 4048\".\n\nNow check if any other issues: The solution uses \"L_i = { (i,j) | 1 ≤ j < p(i) }\" and \"R_i = { (i,j) | p(i) < j ≤ n }\". They define L and R as unions. They claim \"A tile cannot contain squares from both L_i and R_i of the same row, because then the rectangle would have to cross the hole (i,p(i)).\" That's correct.\n\nThe lower bound argument uses a_i = (i, p(i)-1) for rows with p(i)>1. They claim there are exactly n-1 such points (the unique row with p(i)=1 contributes none). That's correct. Then they argue each rectangle can contain at most one a_i. That's correct as we reasoned. Then they claim \"Since there are n-1 points a_i, at least n-1 rectangles are necessary to cover the whole set L.\" As we noted, they didn't explicitly argue that each rectangle must contain at least one a_i, but it's implied because any rectangle covering any part of L must contain the a_i for the row with minimal p(i)-1. However, they didn't state that. So it's a justification gap.\n\nSimilarly for R.\n\nThus Level 3: Justification Gap.\n\nThus final verdict PASS.\n\nNow we need to ensure we adhere to token limit. Our JSON is short.\n\nNow produce final answer."
