&\\text{if }\\sigma(i)=n.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) is the rightmost covered cell in row \\(i\\) that lies to the left of the uncovered cell; \\(R_i\\) is the leftmost covered cell in row \\(i\\) that lies to the right of the uncovered cell.  \n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) (no left‑corner) and exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) (no right‑corner). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{L_i\\}=n-1,\\qquad \\#\\{R_i\\}=n-1,\n\\]\nso the total number of corner cells is  \n\n\\[\nC:=\\{L_i\\mid\\sigma(i)>1\\}\\cup\\{R_i\\mid\\sigma(i)<n\\},\\qquad |C|=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  Lemma – a rectangle contains at most one corner cell  \n\nLet a tile be a rectangle \\(X\\times Y\\) where \\(X\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(Y\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are intervals of rows and columns, respectively.  \nThe tile is admissible only if it does **not** contain any uncovered square, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\forall i\\in X:\\ \\sigma(i)\\notin Y. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe claim that such a tile can contain at most one element of \\(C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that the tile contains two distinct corner cells.\n\n**(i) Two left‑corner cells.**  \nSuppose \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_{i'}\\) belong to the tile, with \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(i')\\) (the other order is symmetric).  \nThen \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')-1\\) are both in \\(Y\\). Because \\(Y\\) is an interval, it also contains every integer between them, in particular \\(\\sigma(i)\\). This contradicts (1) for row \\(i\\).\n\n**(ii) Two right‑corner cells.**  \nSuppose \\(R_i\\) and \\(R_{i'}\\) belong to the tile, with \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(i')\\).  \nThen \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')+1\\) lie in \\(Y\\); the interval \\(Y\\) therefore contains \\(\\sigma(i)\\), contradicting (1) for row \\(i\\).\n\n**(iii) One left‑corner and one right‑corner cell.**  \nAssume \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_{i'}\\) belong to the tile.  \nSince \\(L_i\\) is present, the right endpoint 