y we missed. Let's examine the definition: L_i is the rightmost covered cell left of the uncovered cell in row i. R_i is the leftmost covered cell right of the uncovered cell in row i. So L_i is the cell immediately left of the hole (if exists), and R_i is the cell immediately right of the hole (if exists). So L_i and R_i are adjacent horizontally to the hole. In our counterexample where sigma(i) = sigma(i')+2, L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1) = (i, sigma(i')+1). R_{i'} = (i', sigma(i')+1). So they are in the same column, but not adjacent horizontally to the same hole: L_i is left neighbor of hole (i, sigma(i)), R_{i'} is right neighbor of hole (i', sigma(i')). They are in same column, but they are not adjacent to each other horizontally; they are in different rows.\n\nNow, can a rectangle that includes both L_i and R_{i'} avoid all holes? The rectangle includes column sigma(i')+1. It includes rows i and i'. It also includes any rows between i and i' if they exist. If i' = i+1, then X = {i,i+1}, Y = {sigma(i')+1} = {sigma(i)-1}. The rectangle includes cells (i, sigma(i)-1) and (i+1, sigma(i)-1). Are there any holes in those positions? No, because holes are at (i, sigma(i)) and (i+1, sigma(i+1)). sigma(i+1) = sigma(i') = sigma(i)-2. So hole at row i+1 is at column sigma(i)-2, not sigma(i)-1. So rectangle does not contain holes. So indeed a rectangle can contain both a left-corner and a right-corner cell.\n\nThus the lemma is false. However, maybe the known result is that each rectangle can contain at most one \"corner cell\" of either type, but the definition of corner cell might be different: maybe they consider \"corner cells\" as cells that are either left neighbor of a hole or right neighbor of a hole, but they also require that the rectangle must be maximal in some sense? But the lemma as stated is false.\n\nThus the solution's reasoning is invalid. So Level 2 fails.\n\nNow, answer correctness: The answer may still be correct, but we cannot guarantee. Let's try to see if w