,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nD_i=\\begin{cases}\n(i+1,\\pi(i)), &\\text{if }i<n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{undefined}, &\\text{if }i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) is the unit square immediately to the **right** of the hole in its row, and \\(D_i\\) the square immediately **below** the hole in its column.  \nBecause exactly one hole lies in the last column and exactly one hole lies in the last row, the total number of defined critical cells is  \n\n\\[\n|C|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2,\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{R_i\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\}\\cup\\{D_i\\mid i<n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A rectangle contains at most one critical cell  \n\nLet a rectangle \\(R\\) be the product of a row interval \\([a,b]\\) and a column interval \\([c,d]\\) (with \\(a\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\)).  \nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two distinct critical cells.  \nWe consider three possibilities.\n\n*Case A – two right‑adjacent cells.*  \nSuppose \\(R\\) contains \\((i,\\pi(i)+1)\\) and \\((j,\\pi(j)+1)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(\\pi(i)<\\pi(j)\\).  \nSince both columns \\(\\pi(i)+1\\) and \\(\\pi(j)+1\\) belong to \\([c,d]\\), the whole interval \\([\\pi(i)+1,\\pi(j)+1]\\) is contained in \\([c,d]\\); in particular the column \\(\\pi(j)\\) lies in \\([c,d]\\).  \nBoth rows \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) lie in \\([a,b]\\), hence the square \\((j,\\pi(j))\\) (a hole) belongs to \\(R\\) – a contradiction.\n\n*Case B – two below‑adjacent cells.*  \nThis is symmetric to Case A after swapping the roles of rows and columns; again a hole would be forced inside \\(R\\).\n\n*Case C – one right‑adjacent and one below‑adjacent cell.*  \nLet the right‑adjacent cell be \\((i,\\pi(i)+1)\\) and the below‑adjacent cell be \\((j+1,\\pi(j))\\).\n\n- If \\(i=j\\), then \\(R\\) contains rows \\(i,i+1\\) and columns \\(\\pi(i),\\pi(i)+1\\); consequently the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) lies in \\(R\\).\n\n- If \\(i\\neq j\\), assume \\(i<j+1\\) (otherwise exchange the two cells).  \n  Then the row interval \\([a,b]\\) contains the rows \\(i\\), \\(j\\) and \\(j+1\\).  \n  The column interval \\([c,d]\\) contains the two columns \\(\\pi(i)+1\\) and \\(\\pi(j)\\).  \n  If \\(\\pi(i)+1\\le\\pi(j)\\), then \\(\\pi(j)\\in[c,d]\\) and row \\(j\\in[a,b]\\), so the hole \\((j,\\pi(j))\\) lies in \\(R\\).  \n  If \\(\\pi(j)<\\pi(i)+1\\), then \\(\\pi(i)\\in[c,d]\\) (because \\(\\pi(i)=\\pi(i)+1-1\\) lies between \\(\\pi(j)\\) and \\(\\pi(i)+1\\)), and row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\); thus the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) lies in \\(R\\).\n\nIn every subcase a hole is forced inside the rectangle, contradicting the requirement that tiles avoid all holes.  \nHence **no rectangle can contain two distinct critical cells**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nSince each rectangle can cover at most one critical cell and there are \\(|C|=2n-2\\) critical cells, any tiling of the board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2n-2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the holes are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a **horizontal** rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a **vertical** rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((n-j)\\times 1\\).  \n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every square of the board except the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\).  \nThus the board can be tiled with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangular tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same number \\(2n-2\\).  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
