right of the hole.\n\nAll these tiles are disjoint because each of them occupies a distinct row, and within a row the left and right tiles occupy disjoint column intervals.  \nNone of them contains a diagonal square, so the holes are left uncovered.  \nTogether they cover every non‑diagonal square, i.e. every unit square that is allowed to be covered.\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Optimality\n\nThe lower bound (5) and the explicit construction (6) coincide; therefore the bound is tight.  \nFor the original problem with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe smallest possible number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid while leaving exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
