ecause \\(p\\) is a bijection.)  \nConsider any tile \\(T\\) that contains two distinct left‑adjacent squares, say \\(L_{i_a}\\) and \\(L_{i_b}\\) with \\(a<b\\).  \nLet the row interval of \\(T\\) be \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and the column interval be \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nSince \\(L_{i_a}\\) and \\(L_{i_b}\\) belong to \\(T\\),\n\n\\[\nr_1\\le i_a,i_b\\le r_2,\\qquad \nc_1\\le p(i_a)-1,\\;p(i_b)-1\\le c_2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause of the ordering (2) we have \\(p(i_a)<p(i_b)\\), whence  \n\n\\[\np(i_a)\\le p(i_b)-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) and (4) it follows that the column \\(p(i_a)\\) lies in the interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nThus the square \\((i_a,p(i_a))\\) – which is precisely the hole in row \\(i_a\\) – is covered by \\(T\\), contradicting the definition of a tile.  \n\nHence **no tile can contain two different left‑adjacent squares**.  \nSince there are exactly \\(n-1\\) such squares (all rows except the one with \\(p(i)=1\\)), at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are necessary to cover them.\n\nAn entirely symmetric argument applied to the *right‑adjacent* squares  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,p(i)+1)\\qquad(p(i)<n)\n\\]\n\nshows that each tile can contain at most one of the \\(R_i\\), and there are again \\(n-1\\) of them.  \nTherefore at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles are required.\n\nConsequently any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) (the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal).  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place a tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{L}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,i-1\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle covering the squares left of the hole \\((i,i)\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place a tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{R}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle covering the squares right of the hole.\n\nAll these tiles are disjoint because each of them occupies a distinct row, and within a row the left and right tiles occupy disjoint column intervals.  \nNone of them contains a diagonal square, so the holes are left uncovered.  \nTogether they cover every non‑diagonal square, i.e. every unit square that is allowed to be covered.\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Optimality\n\nThe lower bound (5) and the explicit construction (6) coincide; therefore the bound is tight.  \nFor the original problem with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe smallest possible number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid while leaving exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
