covered squares is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a bijection (a permutation).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) we call \\((i,p(i))\\) the **hole** of that row.\n\nDefine the two regions that must be covered:\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\},\\qquad R_i=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\nThus the set of squares to be tiled is \\(L\\cup R\\); the sets \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint.\n\n#### 2.  Geometry of a tile  \n\nA tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  \nLet a tile \\(T\\) have row interval \\([a,b]\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\)) and column interval \\([c,d]\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\)).  \nBecause \\(T\\) may not contain a hole, for every row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we must have\n\\[\np(i)\\notin[c,d].\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf there exist rows \\(i,i'\\in[a,b]\\) with \\(p(i)\\le d\\) and \\(p(i')\\ge c\\), then \\([c,d]\\) would contain either \\(p(i)\\) or \\(p(i')\\), contradicting (1).  \nHence one of the following holds for the whole interval \\([a,b]\\):\n\n* **Left\u2011type:** \\(d<p(i)\\) for all \\(i\\in[a,b]\\).  \n  The column interval lies completely to the left of every hole in the rows of the tile.\n\n* **Right\u2011type:** \\(c>p(i)\\) for all \\(i\\in[a,b]\\).  \n  The column interval lies completely to the right of every hole in the rows of the tile.\n\nThus **every tile is either a left\u2011tile or a right\u2011tile**.  No tile can contain squares from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\).\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for left\u2011tiles  \n\nConsider a left\u2011tile \\(T\\) with row interval \\(I_T=[a_T,b_T]\\) and column interval \\([c_T,d_T]\\).  \nBecause \\(T\\) is left\u2011type, (1) gives \\(p(i)>d_T\\) for all \\(i\\in I_T\\).  \nDefine\n\\[\ns(T)=\\min\\{p(i):i\\in I_T\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThen \\(s(T)>d_T\\).\n\n*Claim.* The numbers \\(s(T)\\) are distinct for distinct left\u2011tiles.  \n\nIndeed, suppose two left\u2011tiles \\(T\\) and \\(T'\\) satisfy \\(s(T)=s(T')=s\\).  \nBy definition of \\(s\\) there exists a unique row \\(i_0\\) with \\(p(i_0)=s\\).  \nSince \\(s>d_T\\) and \\(s>d_{T'}\\), row \\(i_0\\) belongs to both \\(I_T\\) and \\(I_{T'}\\), contradicting the fact that the interiors of two distinct tiles are disjoint.  Hence the claim holds.\n\nExactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\); all other \\(n-1\\) rows have a non\u2011empty left part \\(L_i\\).  \nConsequently the set \\(\\{s(T)\\}\\) can contain at most the \\(n-1\\) values \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nSince the values are distinct, we must have at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011tiles.\n\n#### 4.  A lower bound for right\u2011tiles  \n\nThe argument is symmetric.  \nFor a right\u2011tile \\(T\\) let\n\\[\nt(T)=\\max\\{p(i):i\\in I_T\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nBecause \\(T\\) is right\u2011type we have \\(t(T)<c_T\\).  \nIf two right\u2011tiles shared the same value of \\(t\\), the unique row whose hole column equals that value would belong to both of their row intervals, impossible.  \nExactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) rows have a non\u2011empty right part.  \nThus there must be at least \\(n-1\\) right\u2011tiles.\n\n#### 5.  Combined lower bound  \n\nEvery admissible tiling consists of left\u2011tiles and right\u2011tiles.  \nFrom Sections\u202f3 and\u202f4 we obtain\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### 6.  A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nFix any permutation \\(p\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) do the following:\n\n* If \\(p(i)>1\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(p(i)-1)\\) covering the squares\n  \\[\n  (i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,p(i)-1).\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n* If \\(p(i)<n\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(n-p(i))\\) covering the squares\n  \\[\n  (i,p(i)+1),(i,p(i)+2),\\dots ,(i,n).\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\nThe two tiles (when both exist) lie on opposite sides of the hole \\((i,p(i))\\); therefore they are disjoint and contain no hole.  \nAll squares of \\(L\\cup R\\) are covered, and the total number of tiles is\n\\[\n\\underbrace{\\#\\{i:p(i)>1\\}}_{=\\,n-1}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{\\#\\{i:p(i)<n\\}}_{=\\,n-1}\n\\;=\\;2n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists for every permutation.\n\n#### 7.  Optimality  \n\nInequality\u202f(4) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while construction\u202f(7) shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the bound is tight: the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
