                    "reasoning_content": "We need to verify the provided solution for the problem: \"Consider a 2025×2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nThe solution says: Let n=2025. The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix. Then define left and right parts L and R. Show each tile must be either left-type or right-type (cannot contain any hole). Then prove lower bound: at least n-1 left-tiles, at least n-1 right-tiles, thus total at least 2n-2 tiles. Then construct a tiling achieving 2n-2 tiles: For each row i, place a tile covering squares left of the hole (if any) and a tile covering squares right of the hole (if any). That yields exactly n-1 left-tiles (since exactly one row has hole at column 1, thus no left tile for that row) and n-1 right-tiles (since exactly one row has hole at column n). So total 2n-2 = 4048 tiles. Thus answer: 4048.\n\nWe need to evaluate according to hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, Level 1: Answer correctness. The answer is 4048. We need to verify if that answer is correct. Let's think: The problem asks for minimum number of tiles needed to have exactly one uncovered square per row and column. The solution's reasoning seems plausible. Let's check if any alternative could achieve fewer tiles. The lower bound argument: at least n-1 left-tiles and at least n-1 right-tiles. Is that correct? Let's examine.\n\nWe have n rows and n columns. Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. So there are n uncovered squares.\n\nDefine L_i = squares left of the hole in row i, R_i = squares right of the hole. So each row i has L_i of s