e rectangle\nmust satisfy one of the two strict inequalities\n\n\\[\nr_{2}<c_{1}\\qquad\\text{(the rectangle lies completely above the diagonal)},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nc_{2}<r_{1}\\qquad\\text{(the rectangle lies completely below the diagonal)}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set of all tiles splits into two independent families:\nthose satisfying (1) (covering the *upper‑triangular* region\n\\(\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\)) and those satisfying (2) (covering the\n*lower‑triangular* region \\(\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\)).  The two families never\nintersect, and together they must cover all off‑diagonal cells.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the upper‑triangular region\n\nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\) the leftmost cell belonging to the\nupper‑triangular region is \\((i,i+1)\\).  Call this cell the *leftmost\ncell of row* \\(i\\).\n\n\\[\n\\text{Leftmost cell of row }i\\;=\\;(i,i+1),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n-1.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\) that satisfies (1).  Write its row and\ncolumn intervals as \\([r_{1},r_{2}]\\) and \\([c_{1},c_{2}]\\) with\n\\(r_{2}<c_{1}\\).  Suppose that \\(R\\) contains two distinct leftmost\ncells \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((i',i'+1)\\) with \\(i<i'\\).  Then\n\n\\[\nr_{1}\\le i\\le r_{2},\\qquad c_{1}\\le i+1\\le c_{2},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\\[\nr_{1}\\le i'\\le r_{2},\\qquad c_{1}\\le i'+1\\le c_{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(r_{2}<c_{1}\\) and \\(i\\le r_{2}\\) we obtain \\(i<c_{1}\\).  Together\nwith \\(c_{1}\\le i+1\\) this forces \\(c_{1}=i+1\\) and \\(r_{2}=i\\).  But\nthen (5) would require \\(i'\\le r_{2}=i\\), contradicting \\(i<i'\\).\nHence **a rectangle above the diagonal can contain at most one leftmost\ncell**.\n\nSince there are \\(n-1\\) distinct leftmost cells (3), at least \\(n-1\\)\nrectangles are necessary to cover the whole upper‑triangular region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the lower‑triangular region\n\nBy symmetry, in the lower‑triangular region the *topmost cell of column*\n\\(j\\;(2\\le j\\le n)\\) is \\((j-1,j)\\).  An argument identical to the one\nabove shows that a rectangle satisfying (2) can contain at most one such\ncell.  Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for the\nlower‑triangular region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Overall lower bound\n\nCombining the two parts we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling uses at least }(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\text{ rectangles}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nDefine the following \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n*For the upper‑triangular region* (rows above the diagonal)  \nfor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle; it lies entirely above the\ndiagonal because its only row index \\(i\\) is smaller than every column\nindex it uses.\n\n*For the lower‑triangular region* (columns below the diagonal)  \nfor each \\(j=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle; it lies entirely below the\ndiagonal because every row index it uses exceeds its column index \\(j\\).\n\n**Disjointness.**  \nAll rectangles \\(H_i\\) occupy distinct rows (each uses exactly row\n\\(i\\)), while all rectangles \\(V_j\\) occupy distinct columns (each uses\nexactly column \\(j\\)).  Moreover a rectangle \\(H_i\\) contains only\ncolumns larger than \\(i\\), whereas a rectangle \\(V_j\\) contains only rows\nlarger than \\(j\\); therefore no cell belongs to both an \\(H_i\\) and a\n\\(V_j\\).  Hence the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n**Coverage.**  \n*If \\(i<j\\)* then the cell \\((i,j)\\) lies in the upper‑triangular\nregion and is covered by \\(H_i\\)