ers diagram of staircase shape \\((n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1)\\).  The same argument applied to \\(R\\) (using the inequality \\(p(i)<j\\)) shows that \\(R\\) has the same shape, reflected about the main diagonal. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Lower bound for \\(L\\))\n\nAny partition of \\(L\\) into axis\u2011parallel rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc_i:=p(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((i,c_i)\\) is the **rightmost** cell of row \\(i\\) that belongs to \\(L\\); we call it a *left corner*.  Because the numbers \\(p(i)-1\\) run through \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) exactly once, the set of left corners is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_L=\\{(i_j,j)\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nwhere each column \\(j\\) contains precisely one left corner.\n\nSuppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) of a partition contains two distinct left corners \\((i,j)\\) and \\((i',j')\\) with \\(j<j'\\).  Since \\(Q\\) is axis\u2011parallel, its column interval contains the whole segment \\([j,j']\\).  In particular, column \\(j'\\) belongs to \\(Q\\) together with row \\(i\\); thus \\((i,j')\\in Q\\).  But \\(j'\\ge p(i)=j+1\\), so \\((i,j')\\) lies **on or to the right** of the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) of row \\(i\\).  Consequently \\((i,j')\\notin L\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(Q\\subseteq L\\).  Hence a rectangle can contain **at most one** left corner.\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal C_L\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct cells, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all of them, and therefore to partition the whole set \\(L\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Lower bound for \\(R\\))\n\nAny partition of \\(R\\) into axis\u2011parallel rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric.  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) define the *right corner* \\((i,p(i)+1)\\).  These \\(n-1\\) cells lie in distinct columns \\(2,\\dots ,n\\).  The same \u201ctwo\u2011corner\u201d contradiction as in Lemma\u202f2 shows that a rectangle can contain at most one right corner, whence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for \\(R\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 1 (Global lower bound)\n\nAny tiling of the board that satisfies the problem\u2019s condition uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n*Proof.*  The covered squares split into the disjoint sets \\(L\\) and \\(R\\).  Tiles covering squares of \\(L\\) cannot contain squares of \\(R\\) (otherwise they would contain a hole).  Hence the tiles used for \\(L\\) and those used for \\(R\\) are disjoint families.  Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f3 give the respective lower bounds, and adding them yields (4). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Construction attaining the bound)\n\nLet the permutation be the identity: \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Place the following rectangles:\n\n* For each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  V_c:=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n  \\(V_c\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\) and lies entirely in \\(L\\).\n\n* For each row \\(r=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  H_r:=\\{(r,j)\\mid j=r+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n  \\(H_r\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times(n-r)\\) and lies entirely in \\(R\\).\n\nThe rectangles \\(\\{V_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{H_r\\}_{r=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, their union equals \\(L\\cup R\\), and none of them contains a diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\).  Hence they form a valid tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nCorollary\u202f1 shows that no admissible tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while Lemma\u202f4 exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
