.**  \n   For each row \\(i\\):\n   - If \\(\\pi(i)>1\\), place a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) (the left segment).  \n   - If \\(\\pi(i)<n\\), place a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((i,\\pi(i)+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) (the right segment).\n\n   Exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) (no left segment) and exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) (no right segment).  Hence the total number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The tiles are disjoint, each lies on grid lines, and together they cover every unit square except the prescribed uncovered ones.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  With \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe board consists of the set  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nHence the uncovered squares form a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation\n\\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is\n\\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\in B\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\in B\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(B\\setminus\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\}=L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\nA rectangular tile cannot contain a hole, therefore every tile is completely\ncontained either in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left region \\(L\\)\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) the *rightmost* cell of its left segment is  \n\n\\[\np_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\in L .\n\\]\n\nConsider any partition of \\(L\\) into axis\u2011parallel rectangles.\nLet \\(\\mathcal{R}_L\\) be the set of rectangles used.\n\n*Claim.* Distinct rows \\(i\\neq i'\\) with \\(\\pi(i),\\pi(i')>1\\) belong to distinct\nrectangles of \\(\\mathcal{R}_L\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nSuppose \\(p_i\\) and \\(p_{i'}\\) lie in the same rectangle \\(R\\in\\mathcal{R}_L\\).\nSince \\(R\\) is a rectangle, its set of columns is an interval\n\\([c_1,c_2]\\) with \\(c_2\\ge \\pi(i)-1\\) (because \\(p_i\\) is in \\(R\\)).\nIf \\(c_2>\\pi(i)-1\\) then column \\(\\pi(i)\\) would also belong to \\(R\\),\nand because row \\(i\\) is in the row interval of \\(R\\), the cell\n\\((i,\\pi(i))\\) \u2013 the hole \u2013 would be inside \\(R\\), a contradiction.\nHence \\(c_2=\\pi(i)-1\\).\n\nThe same reasoning applied to \\(p_{i'}\\) yields \\(c_2=\\pi(i')-1\\).\nThus \\(\\pi(i)-1=\\pi(i')-1\\), i.e. \\(\\pi(i)=\\pi(i')\\), which is impossible\nbecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation.  \u220e\n\nConsequently each row with a non\u2011empty left segment forces a distinct rectangle.\nExactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) (no left segment), so the number of rows with\n\\(\\pi(i)>1\\) is \\(n-1\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{R}_L|\\ge n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right region \\(R\\)\n\nThe argument is symmetric.  \nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) the *leftmost* cell of its right segment is  \n\n\\[\nq_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\in R .\n\\]\n\nIf two such cells lay in the same rectangle of a partition of \\(R\\), the\nleftmost column of that rectangle would have to be simultaneously\n\\(\\pi(i)+1\\) and \\(\\pi(i')+1\\), forcing \\(\\pi(i)=\\pi(i')\\), a contradiction.\nHence each of the \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) yields a distinct rectangle, and\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{R}_R|\\ge n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nEvery tile belongs either to \\(\\mathcal{R}_L\\) or to \\(\\mathcal{R}_R\\); the two\nfamilies are disjoint.  From (1) and (2),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=|\\mathcal{R}_L|+|\\mathcal{R}_R|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling achieving the bound\n\nTake the permutation \\(\\pi\\) that describes the uncovered squares.\nFor each row \\(i\\):\n\n* if \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells\n  \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\);\n* if \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells\n  \\((i,\\pi(i)+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\).\n\nThese tiles are axis\u2011parallel rectangles, they are pairwise disjoint,\nand together they cover every cell of \\(L\\cup R\\).  \nExactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) (no left tile) and exactly one row has\n\\(\\pi(i)=n\\) (no right tile); therefore the total number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (3) and the explicit construction (4) coincide; thus the\nminimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
