e column interval is 2-3, which does not include column 1 (uncovered for row1) nor column 4 (uncovered for row2). So indeed it's possible to have a rectangle that intersects both L and R without covering any uncovered squares, as long as the uncovered columns for the rows are not within the rectangle's column interval. In this case, the uncovered columns are 1 and 4, which are outside the interval [2,3]. So the earlier reasoning that any rectangle mixing left side of row i and right side of row j must include column i or j is false if the uncovered columns are not equal to row indices (i.e., permutation not identity). In general, the uncovered columns are given by permutation π, which can be any permutation. So we need to examine the condition: For rows i and j, with uncovered columns π(i) and π(j). Suppose we want a rectangle covering left side of row i (columns < π(i)) and right side of row j (columns > π(j)). The rectangle's column interval must include some columns less than π(i) and some columns greater than π(j). If π(i) < π(j), then the interval from some column less than π(i) to some column greater than π(j) will include all columns between them, including possibly π(i) and π(j). But if π(i) < π(j), then any interval that includes a column less than π(i) and a column greater than π(j) must include π(i) and π(j) because they lie between. However, we could choose a rectangle that includes left side of row i and right side of row j only if π(i) > π(j). Because then left side columns are < π(i) (which could be > π(j)), and right side columns are > π(j) (which could be < π(i)). If π(i) > π(j), then there is a gap between π(j) and π(i) where we can choose columns that are > π(j) and < π(i). Those columns are not uncovered squares for either row. So rectangle can be placed in that gap. So the existence of such rectangle depends on the relative ordering of π(i) and π(j). So the claim that a tile cannot intersect both L and R is false in general, but may be true for 