tangle, then rectangle's rightmost column would be π(i)-1 and also π(i')-1, forcing π(i)=π(i'), contradiction. This seems correct: a rectangle covering p_i and p_i' would have to include columns up to max of those columns; but if rectangle includes both p_i and p_i', its rightmost column must be at least max(π(i)-1, π(i')-1). However, they argue that if the rectangle's rightmost column > π(i)-1, then column π(i) would be included, which is not allowed. But if the rectangle's rightmost column is exactly π(i)-1, then for p_i' we need π(i')-1 = π(i)-1, so π(i)=π(i'). So indeed distinct rectangles. Similarly for right side.\n\nThus at least n-1 tiles in L and n-1 tiles in R, total 2n-2.\n\nConstruction: they propose horizontal tiles covering left side and right side of each row. That yields exactly n-1 left tiles (since one row has π(i)=1, no left tile) and n-1 right tiles (one row has π(i)=n). So total 2n-2 tiles. The construction respects no overlapping because each tile is in a distinct row. So it's valid.\n\nThus answer appears correct.\n\nThus Level 1: answer is correct.\n\nLevel 2: Check reasoning validity. The solution uses valid mathematical methods: combinatorial reasoning, case analysis, counting, construction, proof by contradiction (implicitly). The solution includes a claim about distinct rectangles, proven by contradiction. The method of splitting into left and right regions and counting distinct tiles is valid. The construction uses explicit specification: \"horizontal tile covering cells (i,1) to (i,π(i)-1)\" etc. This is concrete details (Category C). So reasoning uses valid methods.\n\nThus Level 2: valid.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to check for justification gaps or critical errors.\n\nPotential issues: The solution's claim that each tile is completely contained either in L or R is justified by \"cannot contain uncovered square\". That's fine. The claim that each tile belongs either to L or R is correct because the tile cannot intersect both sets (since L and R are separated by the uncovered squares). However, we need to confirm that L and R are indeed separated: L includes cells left of uncovered squares, R includes cells right of uncovered squares. Since uncovered squares are at positions (i,π(i)), L contains cells with column < π(i), R contains cells with column > π(i). So any tile that contains cells from both sides would have to cross the column π(i) for some row i, which would include the uncovered square (if tile includes that column for that row). But could a tile be placed that includes cells from left side of some rows and right side of other rows, but not the uncovered squares? For example, a tile that spans multiple rows could have columns that are left of uncovered squares for some rows and right of uncovered squares for other rows, but not include any uncovered squares because the uncovered squares are at different columns per row. However, the solution's argument that a tile cannot intersect both L and R might be flawed: Suppose we have a rectangle covering rows i and i', columns from a to b. If for row i, uncovered square is at column π(i) > b, then the rectangle lies entirely left of that uncovered square (in L). If for row i', uncovered square is at column π(i') < a, then rectangle lies entirely right of that uncovered square (in R). But the rectangle includes both rows i and i', and columns a..b. For row i, the rectangle is left side; for row i', it's right side. However, the rectangle does not contain any uncovered square because for each row, the uncovered square is outside the rectangle. So the rectangle would intersect both L and R sets (since L and R are defined as union over rows of left and right cells). Indeed, L∪R = all cells except uncovered squares. So a rectangle that covers cells from both left side of some rows and right side of other rows would be a subset of L∪R but intersect both L and R. The solution says \"a tile cannot intersect both sets\". Is that true?