a gives 2n-2 = 2. So minimum tiles predicted = 2. Is it possible with 1 tile? Let's test: For uncovered squares (1,1) and (2,2), the covered cells are (1,2) and (2,1). These are two cells not adjacent (they are not forming a rectangle because they are not contiguous in a rectangle shape). A rectangle covering both would need to include (1,1) or (2,2) or also (1,1) and (2,2) because rectangle covering (1,2) and (2,1) would be the whole 2x2 grid, which includes uncovered squares. So can't use 1 tile. So need at least 2 tiles, each covering one cell. So 2 is correct for n=2.\n\nNow n=3. Let's test if we can do less than 2n-2 = 4. For n=3, we need 3 uncovered squares, one per row and column. Suppose uncovered squares are at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Then covered cells are all other cells: 9-3=6 cells. Could we cover them with 3 tiles? Possibly. Let's try to cover them with 3 rectangles: maybe one rectangle covering row 1 columns 2-3 (cells (1,2),(1,3)), one rectangle covering row 2 columns 1-3 except (2,2) maybe two rectangles? Actually row 2 left side (2,1) and right side (2,3). Could we combine (2,1) with (1,2) in a rectangle? That rectangle would need to be rows 1-2, columns 1-2? That includes (1,1) uncovered, (2,2) uncovered. Not allowed. Could we combine (2,3) with (3,2) etc? Might be tricky.\n\nBut maybe there is a construction with 3 tiles: For each row i, we have left block and right block. For row 1 left block empty, right block (1,2)-(1,3) can be a tile. Row 2 left block (2,1) and right block (2,3) are separate cells; could we combine them into one tile covering (2,1)-(2,3) but that includes (2,2) uncovered, not allowed. So need separate tiles for left and right. Row 3 left block (3,1)-(3,2) can be a tile. So we have 4 tiles: row1 right, row2 left, row2 right, row3 left. That's 4 tiles. So matches formula.\n\nBut could we use a tile covering (2,1) and (3,1) (vertical) maybe? Let's see: (2,1) and (3,1) are left side cells for rows 2 and 3. Could we have a vertical tile covering rows 2-3, column 1? That would be a rectangle covering (2,1) and (3,1). Does it intersect any uncovered squares? Uncovered squares are (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). The tile (col 1, rows 2-3) does not include (1,1) because row 1 not included, does not include (2,2) because column 2 not included, does not include (3,3). So it's allowed. So we could combine left side cells of rows 2 and 3 into one tile. Similarly, we could combine right side cells of rows 1 and 2 maybe? Right side cells are (1,2),(1,3) and (2,3). Could we have a rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 2-3? That rectangle includes (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3). But (2,2) is uncovered, so not allowed. So can't combine those.\n\nBut we could combine left side cells of rows 2 and 3 into one tile, reducing tile count by 1. Then we would have: tile covering (2,1),(3,1); tile covering (3,2) maybe combined with something else? (3,2) is left side cell for row 3 (since π(3)=3). Actually left side cells for row 3 are columns 1 and 2. But we already used (3,1) in the vertical tile. So left side cell (3,2) remains uncovered. Could we combine (3,2) with (2,3) maybe? That would be rectangle rows 2-3, columns 2-3, includes (2,2) uncovered, not allowed. Could combine (3,2) with (1,2) maybe vertical tile column 2 rows 1-3? That includes (2,2) uncovered, not allowed. So (3,2) must be covered by its own tile (or combine with something else that doesn't include uncovered squares). Could combine (3,2) with (1,3) maybe rectangle rows 1-3, columns 2-3? That includes (2,2) uncovered. So not allowed.\n\nThus we still need tile for (3,2). So total tiles: vertical tile covering (2,1),(3,1); tile covering (1,2),(1,3); tile covering (2,3); tile covering (3,2). That's 4 tiles again. So no reduction.\n\nBut maybe we could combine right side cells of rows 1 and 3? Right side cells: row1: (1,2),(1,3); row3: (3,4?) Actually row3 right side cells are columns >π(3)=3, i.e., columns 4...n, but n=3, so none. So no.\n\nThus for n=3, 4 tiles seems minimal. So formula holds.\n\nBut the argument that each tile belongs either to L or R is not correct in general, but maybe the lower bound can still hold even if some tiles intersect both L and R. Let's examine more carefully: The lower bound argument uses the distinctness of rectangles covering p_i and q_i. It says that each p_i must be in a distinct rectangle of \\mathcal{R}_L (the set of rectangles that lie in L). But if a rectangle containing p_i also contains some q_j, then that rectangle is not wholly in L, so it's not counted in \\mathcal{R}_L. However, the lower bound argument only counts rectangles wholly in L. If we allow rectangles that intersect both L and R, they are not counted in either \\mathcal{R}_L or \\mathcal{R}_R. So the total number of tiles could be less than (n-1)+(n-1) if we can use some tiles that intersect both L and R, covering some p_i and some q_j simultaneously, thereby reducing the count of tiles wholly in L or R. The lower bound argument would then be invalid because it assumes all tiles are either wholly in L or wholly in R, which is false.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound is flawed. However, we need to check if there is any other argument that ensures that any tile intersecting both L and R must contain an uncovered square. Let's examine the definitions: L = union over rows of cells left of uncovered squares. R = union over rows of cells right of uncovered squares. L and R partition the set of all cells except uncovered squares. So any cell is either left or right of its row's uncovered square. So any tile that contains cells from both L and R must contain at least one cell that is left of its row's uncovered square and at least one cell that is right of its row's uncovered square. But those cells belong to different rows. So the tile must span multiple rows. For each row i that tile covers, the tile's column interval must be either entirely left of π(i) (if tile's columns are all < π(i)), or entirely right of π(i) (if all > π