g more than one row and more than one column will include a diagonal zero if it includes any row i and column i simultaneously. However, we could have rectangles that are \"row i, all columns except column i\" (i.e., row i covering all columns except column i). That's a rectangle: row i is a single row, columns from 1 to n, but includes column i which is the zero. Actually row i covering all columns includes column i, which is a zero. So not allowed. So we need to avoid column i for row i. So we can have rectangle covering row i, columns 1 to i-1 (left side) and rectangle covering row i, columns i+1 to n (right side). That's two rectangles per row, total 2n rectangles, but we can combine left side rectangles across rows if they don't intersect diagonal zeros. For left side, rectangle covering rows i and j, columns up to min(i-1,j-1) maybe? But if rows i and j have different i and j, the rectangle may include a diagonal zero at (i,i) or (j,j) if column index equals row index for either row. For left side rectangle covering rows i and j, columns up to k, we need to ensure that for each row r in {i,j}, column k < π(r) = r (since π is identity). So we need k < min(i,j). So we could have rectangle covering rows i and j, columns 1..min(i,j)-1. That rectangle avoids diagonal zeros because for each row r, column indices are less than r, so they are not diagonal. So we can combine left side cells across rows. Similarly for right side cells across rows, we can combine rows i and j with columns max(i,j)+1..n. So we can reduce number of rectangles. Indeed, we can cover all left side cells with n-1 rectangles: for each k from 1 to n-1, rectangle covering rows k+1..n, columns 1..k. That yields n-1 rectangles covering all left side cells. Similarly, right side cells with n-1 rectangles: for each k from 2 to n, rectangle covering rows 1..k-1, columns k..n. That yields n-1 rectangles. So total 2n-2 rectangles. This matches the solution's bound. Could we do fewer? Possibly combine left and right side cells in same rectangle across rows, but that would require rectangle covering both left and right columns for some rows, which would include the diagonal zero for those rows (since the rectangle would span columns less than row index and greater than row index, thus includes column equal to row index). So not allowed. So any rectangle that includes both left and right side cells for a given row must include the uncovered square for that row, which is forbidden. However, a rectangle could include left side cells for some rows and right side cells for other rows, as we argued earlier. That could potentially reduce the number of rectangles. For example, a rectangle covering rows i and j, columns from left side of row i (i.e., columns < i) and right side of row j (columns > j). But the rectangle's column interval must be contiguous. So if we try to combine left side of row i (columns < i) and right side of row j (columns > j), the column interval would need to include columns from < i up to > j, which includes column i (if i <= j) or column j (if j <= i). So it would include the uncovered square for whichever row has column index within the interval. Let's examine: Suppose i < j. Row i's left side columns are 1..i-1. Row j's right side columns are j+1..n. To combine them in a rectangle, we need a column interval that includes both sets: at minimum from 1 to n (or at least from 1 to n). That includes column i and column j, which are uncovered squares for rows i and j respectively. So not allowed. If we try to combine left side of row i and left side of row j, we can have column interval up to min(i-1, j-1). That's okay. Similarly for right side of rows i and j, column interval from max(i+1, j+1) to n. So combining left side cells across rows is okay, and combining right side cells across rows is okay. But mixing left side of some rows with right side of other rows seems to cause the rectangle to cross the diagonal for at least one of those rows. Let's 