overed squares. However, the solution's lower bound argument uses that each left cell p_i = (i, pi(i)-1) must be in a distinct rectangle among left region tiles, because if two such cells were in same rectangle, that rectangle's rightmost column would be pi(i)-1 and also pi(i')-1, forcing pi(i)=pi(i'), contradiction. So each left cell must be in a distinct rectangle. That yields at least n-1 left tiles (since one row has pi(i)=1, no left cell). Similarly at least n-1 right tiles. So total at least 2n-2.\n\nNow, is it possible to have a tile covering multiple rows in left region? The argument says no, because each left cell p_i is the rightmost cell in its row left of uncovered square. If a rectangle includes p_i and p_j (i != j), then its rightmost column must be both pi(i)-1 and pi(j)-1, forcing equality, impossible. So each left cell must be in a distinct rectangle, but could a rectangle contain other cells left of uncovered squares besides p_i? Possibly yes, but that doesn't reduce count because each rectangle can contain at most one p_i. So we need at least n-1 left rectangles. Similarly right.\n\nThus lower bound seems correct. Construction: For each row i, create a left tile covering columns 1..pi(i)-1 if pi(i)>1, and a right tile covering columns pi(i)+1..n if pi(i)<n. That's exactly n-1 left tiles (since exactly one row has pi(i)=1) and n-1 right tiles (since exactly one row has pi(i)=n). So total 2n-2. Tiles are disjoint (different rows). So construction works.\n\nThus answer 4048 is correct.\n\nThus Level 1: answer correct.\n\nNow Level 2: Check reasoning validity. The solution uses case analysis, counting arguments, proof by contradiction (in claim). It also uses the method of constructing specific tiles. The solution includes a claim about distinct rectangles for left cells, with proof by contradiction. This is a valid method. The solution also uses the concept of permutation and partition of board into left and right region. All methods are recognized v