(i,j)\\) denotes the square in row \\(i\\) and column \\(j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nMatilda requires that each row and each column contain **exactly one** uncovered square.  \nHence there are exactly \\(n\\) uncovered squares, one in each row and one in each column.  \nConsequently the uncovered squares are at positions  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a bijection (a permutation).  \nFor the remainder of the proof we fix such a permutation \\(p\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) the uncovered square is at column \\(p(i)\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\in B\\mid j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j)\\in B\\mid j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) consists of all squares **to the left** of the uncovered square in their row,\nand \\(R\\) consists of all squares **to the right** of it.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\nB\\setminus U = L\\cup R,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile cannot contain any uncovered square; therefore a tile is either\nentirely contained in \\(L\\) (a *left‑tile*) or entirely in \\(R\\) (a *right‑tile).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for left‑tiles\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,p(i)-1)\n\\]\n\nis the **rightmost** cell of \\(L\\) in that row.  \nIf \\(p(i)=1\\) the row has no cell in \\(L\\).\n\nConsider a left‑tile \\(T\\).  \nLet its set of rows be a consecutive interval \\([a,b]\\) and its set of columns be\na consecutive interval \\([c,d]\\).  \nSince \\(T\\subseteq L\\), every row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) satisfies \\(d<p(i)\\); equivalently\n\n\\[\nd\\le \\min_{i\\in[a,b]}p(i)-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the columns of \\(T\\) end at \\(d\\), the tile can contain