ion ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The board consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation\n\nEach row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square.  \nHence there is a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Four quadrant numbers\n\nFix a row \\(i\\).  The uncovered square in this row is at column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nFor a column \\(j\\) let \\(\\tau(j)=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\) be the row of its uncovered square.\n\nDefine\n\n* \\(a_i=\\#\\{j<\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)>i\\}\\) – squares left of \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) that lie **above** the uncovered square of their column (NW quadrant);\n* \\(b_i=\\#\\{j<\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)<i\\}\\) – squares left of \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) that lie **below** the uncovered square of their column (SW quadrant);\n* \\(c_i=\\#\\{j>\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)>i\\}\\) – squares right of \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) that lie above (NE quadrant);\n* \\(d_i=\\#\\{j>\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)<i\\}\\) – squares right of \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) that lie below (SE quadrant).\n\nAll four numbers are non‑negative integers.  Counting the squares in the four obvious ways yields the relations\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na_i+b_i &= \\sigma(i)-1,\\\\\nc_i+d_i &= n-\\sigma(i),\\\\\na_i+c_i &= n-i,\\\\\nb_i+d_i &= i-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom the last two equalities we obtain\n\n\\[\nc_i=(n-i)-a_i,\\qquad d_i=(i-1)-b_i.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThus the four families of squares are completely described by the two sequences\n\n\\[\nA=\\{a_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad B=\\{b_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  