"### Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous tiling that attains the lower bound, but I have proved a strong necessary bound. In particular, I have shown that any placement of tiles satisfying the conditions must use at least  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{176}\n\\]\n\ntiles. Moreover, I describe a concrete way to choose the uncovered squares (i.e. a permutation of the rows) for which the lower bound can be achieved; the existence of a tiling with exactly \\(176\\) tiles follows from a standard decomposition of Ferrers diagrams into rectangles. The full verification that the four regions indeed decompose as claimed is left as a sketch, so the answer is a **partial solution**: the lower bound is proved rigorously, and a construction attaining it is outlined.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   The uncovered unit squares must form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(\\sigma\\colon\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n2. **Four quadrant numbers.**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) define  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   a_i&=\\#\\{j<\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)>i\\},\\\\\n   b_i&=\\#\\{j<\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)<i\\},\\\\\n   c_i&=\\#\\{j>\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)>i\\},\\\\\n   d_i&=\\#\\{j>\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)<i\\},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\tau=\\sigma^{-1}\\).  \n   The four numbers satisfy\n   \\[\n   a_i+b_i=\\sigma(i)-1,\\qquad c_i+d_i=n-\\sigma(i),\\qquad \n   a_i+c_i=n-i,\\qquad b_i+d_i=i-1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sets of distinct values.**  \n   Let  \n   \\[\n   A=\\{a_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n   B=\\{b_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n   From the first equation of (1) we have \\(\\sigma(i)-1=a_i+b_i\\).  \n   Since the numbers \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) run through \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), the sum\u2011set \\(A+B\\) must contain the whole interval \\([0,n-1]\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   |A|\\cdot|B|\\ge n. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A numeric lower bound.**  \n   By AM\u2013GM,\n   \\[\n   |A|+|B|\\ge 2\\sqrt{n}=2\\cdot45=90. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The four regions of the board (NW,\u202fSW,\u202fNE,\u202fSE) are respectively Ferrers diagrams whose row\u2011length multisets are \\(\\{a_i\\},\\{b_i\\},\\{c_i\\},\\{d_i\\}\\).  \n   A Ferrers diagram whose distinct positive row lengths are \\(k\\) can be tiled with exactly \\(k\\) rectangles (one for each distinct length). Hence the four regions need at least\n   \\[\n   (|A|-1)+(|B|-1)+(|B|-1)+(|A|-1)=2(|A|+|B|)-4 \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   rectangles.  \n   Using (3) in (4) gives the universal lower bound\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot90-4=176 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Take  \n   \\[\n   A=\\{0,1,\\dots ,44\\},\\qquad \n   B=\\{0,45,90,\\dots ,45\\cdot44\\}.\n   \\]\n   Every integer \\(L\\in[0,2024]\\) can be written uniquely as \\(L=a+45b\\) with \\(a\\in A,\\;b\\in B\\).  \n   For the row whose uncovered square must satisfy \\(\\sigma(i)-1=L\\) set \\(a_i=a,\\;b_i=b\\).  \n   Because \\(a\\le44\\) and \\(b\\le45\\cdot44\\), the constraints \\(a_i\\le n-i\\) and \\(b_i\\le i-1\\) can be satisfied by ordering the rows so that the \\(a_i\\)\u2019s are non\u2011increasing (rows with larger \\(a_i\\) placed higher) and the \\(b_i\\)\u2019s are non\u2011decreasing (rows with larger \\(b_i\\) placed lower). This ordering respects the inequalities, and the resulting \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation (the values \\(\\sigma(i)=a_i+b_i+1\\) are all distinct).  \n\n   For this permutation the four regions are Ferrers diagrams whose distinct positive row lengths are exactly the elements of \\(A\\) (for NW and SE) and of \\(B\\) (for SW and NE). Therefore each region can be tiled with \\(|A|-1=44\\) or \\(|B|-1=44\\) rectangles, respectively, giving a total of  \n\n   \\[\n   44+44+44+44=176\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles, i.e. tiles.\n\n   Hence a placement with exactly \\(176\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe board consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, there is a bijection  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Quadrant numbers\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) write \\(\\tau=\\sigma^{-1}\\) (so \\(\\tau(j)\\) is the row of the uncovered square in column \\(j\\)).  \nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na_i&=\\#\\{j<\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)>i\\},\\\\\nb_i&=\\#\\{j<\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)<i\\},\\\\\nc_i&=\\#\\{j>\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)>i\\},\\\\\nd_i&=\\#\\{j>\\sigma(i)\\mid \\tau(j)<i\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\"
