cause the columns are distinct, the column interval also contains the column \\(p(i)\\) together with row \\(i\\), forcing the tile to contain the forbidden hole \\((i,p(i))\\).  The same argument excludes two “left’’ squares.  \n   – If a tile contained an “above’’ square \\((i-1,p(i))\\) and a “left’’ square \\((j,p(j)-1)\\), the row interval would contain rows \\(i-1\\) and \\(j\\); consequently it contains row \\(i\\).  The column interval contains columns \\(p(i)\\) and \\(p(j)-1\\); because the columns are distinct it also contains column \\(p(i)\\).  Hence the tile would again contain the hole \\((i,p(i))\\).  All cases lead to a contradiction. ∎\n\n   Since each tile can cover at most one element of \\(S\\) and \\(|S|=2n-2\\), any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n3. The construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower‑bound proof shows that fewer tiles are impossible.  Therefore the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) the minimum is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nThe condition “each column also contains exactly one uncovered square’’ means that \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound – a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe may choose the permutation \\(p\\) to be the identity, i.e. the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n(Any other permutation can be obtained from the identity by permuting rows and columns; the same number of tiles will be needed, because a permutation of rows or columns merely relabels the grid.)\n\nDefine \\(f(m)\\) as the minimum number of rectangular tiles needed to tile the complement of the diagonal in an \\(m\\times m\\) board.  \nWe prove by induction that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(m)=2m-2\\qquad(m\\ge 1)} .\n\\]\n\n*Base case.*  For \\(m=1\\) the whole board consists of the single uncovered square, so no tile is needed: \\(f(1)=0=2\\cdot1-2\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  Let \\(m>1\\) and split the index set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) into two contiguous halves  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{1,\\dots ,\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{\\lceil m/2\\rceil,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the two rectangles  \n\n\\[\nT_{1}=L\\times R,\\qquad T_{2}=R\\times L .\n\\]\n\nBecause the row‑set of \\(T_{1}\\) is \\(L\\) and its column‑set is \\(R\\), we have \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\); consequently none of the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\) lies in \\(T_{1}\\).  The same holds for \\(T_{2}\\).  Thus both \\(T_{1}\\) and \\(T_{2}\\) are admissible tiles.\n\nAfter placing \\(T_{1}\\) and \\(T_{2}\\) the remaining uncovered squares are exactly the complement of the diagonal inside the two smaller boards \\(L\\times L\\) and \\(R\\times R\\).  By the induction hypothesis these two sub‑boards can be tiled with \\(f(|L|)\\) and \\(f(|R|)\\) tiles, respectively.  Hence\n\n\\[\nf(m)=2+f\\!\\bigl(|L|\\bigr)+f\\!\\bigl(|R|\\bigr).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|L|=\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\) and \\(|R|=\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\), substituting the induction hypothesis \\(f(k)=2k-2\\) for the right‑hand side of (1) yields\n\n\\[\nf(m)=2+\\bigl(2\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor-2\\bigr)+\\bigl(2\\lceil m/2\\rceil-2\\bigr)\n     =2\\bigl(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor+\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\bigr)-2\n     =2m-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus (1) holds for all \\(m\\), completing the induction.\n\nFor \\(m=n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently there exists a placement of **4048** rectangular tiles satisfying all requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nRecall the permutation \\(p\\) (the positions of the uncovered squares).  \nDefine the following set of covered squares:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{A}&=\\{\\, (i-1,\\,p(i)) \\mid 2\\le i\\le n \\,\\}, \\\\\nS_{B}&=\\{\\, (i,\\,p(i)-1) \\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)>1 \\,\\},\\\\[2mm]\nS   &=S_{A}\\cup S_{B}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Explanation of the elements.*  \n- For each row \\(i>1\\) the square \\((i-1,p(i))\\) lies directly **above** the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n- For each row whose uncovered square is not in column 1, the square \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) lies directly **to the left** of that uncovered square.  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square, all squares of \\(S\\) are indeed covered squares.  \nCounting them:\n\n- There are \\(n-1\\) squares in \\(S_{A}\\) (one for each row except the first).  \n- Exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\); that row contributes no element to \\(S_{B}\\).  All other \\(n-1\\) rows contribute a left‑neighbor square.  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=|S_{A}|+|S_{B}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma.** *No axis‑aligned rectangular tile can contain two distinct squares of \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a rectangle \\(R\\) be defined by a contiguous set of rows \\(I\\) and a contiguous set of columns \\(J\\) (so \\(R=I\\times J\\)).  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(R\\) contains two different squares of \\(S\\).  Three possibilities arise.\n\n1. **Both squares belong to \\(S_{A}\\).**  \n   Then they are \\((i-1,p(i))\\) and \\((j-1,p(j))\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \n   Without loss of generality \\(i<j\\).  \n   Since the row‑interval \\(I\\) contains rows \\(i-1\\) and \\(j-1\\), it also contains row \\(i\\).  \n   The column‑interval \\(J\\) contains columns \\(p(i)\\) and \\(p(j)\\); because \\(p(i)\\neq p(j)\\), \\(J\\) also contains the column \\(p(i)\\).  \n   Consequently the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) lies in \\(R\\), contradicting the rule that a tile may not cover an uncovered square.\n\n2. **Both squares belong to \\(S_{B}\\).**  \n   They are \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\((j,p(j)-1)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \n   Again assume \\(i<j\\).  \n   The row‑interval contains rows \\(i\\) and \\(j\\); the column‑interval contains columns \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(j)-1\\).  \n   Because the columns are distinct, the interval also contains the column \\(p(i)\\).  \n   Hence the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) lies inside the rectangle – a contradiction.\n\n3. **One square from \\(S_{A}\\) and one from \\(S_{B}\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(R\\) contains \\((i-1,p(i))\\) (an “above’’ square) and \\((j,p(j)-1)\\) (a “left’’ square) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \n   If \\(i\\le j\\), then the row‑interval contains rows \\(i-1\\) and \\(j\\), and therefore also row \\(i\\).  \n   The column‑interval contains columns \\(p(i)\\) and \\(p(j)-1\\); because the columns are distinct, it also contains column \\(p(i)\\).  \n   Thus the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) belongs to \\(R\\), again impossible.  \n   The case \\(j<i\\) is symmetric.\n\nIn every case we obtain a contradiction; therefore a rectangle cannot contain two distinct elements of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause each tile can cover **at most one** element of the set \\(S\\) and, by (2), \\(|S|=2n-2\\), any tiling of the board must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\text{number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;|S|=2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe construction of Section 1 provides a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the argument of Section 2 shows that no tiling can use fewer tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient."
