rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nConsider a $2025\\times2025$ grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Upper bound – a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe may place the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. we assume  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad\\text{for all }i .\n\\]\n\n(Any other permutation can be obtained from the diagonal by permuting rows and columns, which does not change the number of tiles.)\n\nDefine \\(f(m)\\) to be the minimum number of axis‑aligned rectangular tiles needed to tile the complement of the diagonal in an \\(m\\times m\\) board.  \nWe prove by induction that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(m)=2m-2\\qquad(m\\ge 1)} .\n\\]\n\n*Base case.*  For \\(m=1\\) the board consists solely of the uncovered square, so no tile is needed: \\(f(1)=0=2\\cdot1-2\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  Let \\(m>1\\) and split the index set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) into two contiguous halves  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{1,\\dots ,\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{\\lceil m/2\\rceil,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the two rectangles  \n\n\\[\nT_{1}=L\\times R,\\qquad T_{2}=R\\times L .\n\\]\n\nSince the row‑set of \\(T_{1}\\) is \\(L\\) and its column‑set is \\(R\\), we have \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\); consequently none of the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) lies in \\(T_{1}\\).  The same holds for \\(T_{2}\\).  Thus both \\(T_{1}\\) and \\(T_{2}\\) are admissible tiles.\n\nAfter placing \\(T_{1}\\) and \\(T_{2}\\) the uncovered squares that remain are exactly the diagonal squares inside the two smaller boards \\(L\\times L\\) and \\(R\\times R\\).  By the induction hypothesis these sub‑boards can be tiled with \\(f(|L|)\\) and \\(f(|R|)\\) tiles, respectively.  Hence\n\n\\[\nf(m)=2+f\\!\\bigl(|L|\\bigr)+f\\!\\bigl(|R|\\bigr)\n     =2+f\\!\\bigl(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\bigr)+f\\!\\bigl(\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\bigr).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nUsing the induction hypothesis \\(f(k)=2k-2\\) for the smaller arguments, (1) yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(m)&=2+\\bigl(2\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor-2\\bigr)+\\bigl(2\\lceil m/2\\rceil-2\\bigr)\\\\\n    &=2\\bigl(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor+\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\bigr)-2\n      =2m-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus (1) holds for all \\(m\\), completing the induction.\n\nFor the original board (\\(m=n=2025\\)) we obtain\n\n\\[\nf(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence there exists a placement of **4048** rectangular tiles satisfying the required condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nRecall the permutation \\(p\\) defined above.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define the square directly **above** the uncovered square in that row:\n\n\\[\na_i=(i-1,\\;p(i)).\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the square directly **below** the uncovered square in that row:\n\n\\[\nb_i=(i+1,\\;p(i)).\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{a_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}\\cup\\{b_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness of the elements of \\(S\\).*  \nThe rows of the \\(a_i\\)’s are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\); the rows of the \\(b_i\\)’s are \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\).  \nIf a square belonged to both families we would have\n\\[\n(i-1,\\;p(i))=(j+1,\\;p(j))\n\\]\nfor some \\(i,j\\).  This would imply \\(i=j+2\\) and \\(p(i)=p(j)\\), contradicting the fact that \\(p\\) is a permutation.  Hence the two families are disjoint, and because the columns are the distinct values \\(p(i)\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|S|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery square of \\(S\\) is not an uncovered square (its column is \\(p(i)\\) but its row differs from the row of the hole in that column), therefore each such square must be covered by some tile.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma.** *No axis‑aligned rectangular tile can contain two distinct squares of \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  Let a rectangle be defined by a contiguous set of rows \\(I\\) and a contiguous set of columns \\(J\\) (so the rectangle is \\(I\\times J\\)).  Consider three possibilities.\n\n1. **Both squares are of the form \\(a_i\\).**  \n   Let the squares be \\(a_i=(i-1,p(i))\\) and \\(a_j=(j-1,p(j))\\) with \