y lie in distinct rows, they cover every square except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones, and their sides lie on grid lines.  \n\nThe number of non‑empty left intervals equals the number of rows with \\(p(i)>1\\), i.e. \\(n-1\\).  \nSimilarly the number of non‑empty right intervals is \\(n-1\\).  \nHence the construction uses exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define the **adjacent squares**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i'&=(i,p(i)-1)\\quad\\text{if }p(i)>1,\\\\\nR_i'&=(i,p(i)+1)\\quad\\text{if }p(i)<n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) (no left‑adjacent square) and exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\) (no right‑adjacent square).  \nThus there are precisely  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nadjacent squares in total.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma  \nIn any admissible tiling, a single rectangular tile contains **at most one** adjacent square.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a tile \\(T\\) be a rectangle with rows \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and columns \\(c\\le j\\le d\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) may not contain any uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\), for each row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we must have either\n\n\\[\nd < p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile lies to the left of the hole in row }i\\text{)},\n\\]\n\nor\n\n\\[\nc > p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile lies to the right of the hole in row }i\\text{)} .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll rows in the tile satisfy the same alternative, because otherwise the hole of a row would lie inside \\(T\\).\n\n*Two left‑adjacent squares.*  \nAssume \\(T\\) contains two distinct left‑adjacent squares \\(L_i'=(i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\(L_j'=(j,p(j)-1)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nBoth rows must satisfy the left case of (1), so \\(d\\le p(i)-1\\) and \\(d\\le p(j)-1\\).  \nSince \\(L_i'\\in T\\) we have \\(c\\le p(i)-1\\le d\\); similarly \\(c\\le p(j)-1\\le d\\).  \nConsequently \\(d\\ge\\max\\{p(i)-1,p(j)-1\\}\\).  \nThus \\(d=\\min\\{p(i)-1,p(j)-1\\}=\\max\\{p(i)-1,p(j)-1\\}\\), which forces \\(p(i)=p(j)\\), contradicting that \\(p\\) is a permutation.  \nHence a tile cannot contain two left‑adjacent squares.\n\n*Two right‑adjacent squares.*  \nThe same argument applied to the right case of (1) shows that a tile cannot contain two right‑adjacent squares.\n\n*One left‑ and one right‑adjacent square.*  \nIf a tile contained a left‑adjacent square \\(L_i'\\) it would have to satisfy the left case of (1) for row \\(i\\); if it also contained a right‑adjacent square \\(R_j'\\) it would have to satisfy the right case of (1) for row \\(j\\).  \nA single rectangle cannot simultaneously satisfy both alternatives, so this is impossible.\n\nTherefore any tile contains at most one adjacent square. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Consequence of the Lemma  \n\nThere are \\(2n-2\\) adjacent squares, and each tile can cover at most one of them.  \nHence any tiling must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nIf a tile covered none of the adjacent squares, the total number of tiles would be even larger, so the bound remains valid.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of §1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lemma of §2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
