 the explicit construction with \\(n\\) tiles coincide; thus the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nNumber the rows and columns of the grid by \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reformulation as a matrix problem  \n\nDefine an \\(n\\times n\\) matrix \\(M=(m_{i,j})\\) by  \n\n\\[\nm_{i,j}= \\begin{cases}\n0 &\\text{if the unit square in row }i\\text{ and column }j\\text{ is left uncovered},\\\\[2mm]\n1 &\\text{if that square is covered by a tile}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square,\nthe set of zero entries of \\(M\\) consists of exactly one entry in each row\nand each column.  Hence the zero‑pattern of \\(M\\) is a permutation matrix.\nLet \\(P\\) denote that permutation matrix; i.e. \\(P_{i,j}=1\\) iff the\nuncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies in column \\(j\\).  Let \\(J\\) be the\n\\(n\\times n\\) all‑ones matrix.  Then\n\n\\[\nM = J - P .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nSuppose a placement of tiles satisfies the conditions.  \nFor each tile \\(T\\) let \\(R(T)\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of rows\noccupied by \\(T\\) and \\(C(T)\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the set of columns\noccupied by \\(T\\).  The indicator matrix of \\(T\\) is\n\n\\[\nM_T = \\mathbf{1}_{R(T)}\\,\\mathbf{1}_{C(T)}^{\\!\\top},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{1}_{R(T)}\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf{1}_{C(T)}\\)) is the column\nvector whose entries are \\(1\\) exactly on the rows (resp. columns) of the\ntile and \\(0\\) elsewhere.  Hence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M_T)=1\\).\n\nThe tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover precisely the\n\\(1\\)-entries of \\(M\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nM=\\sum_{T} M_T .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nRank is sub‑additive, so from (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le \\sum_T \\operatorname{rank}(M_T)\n               = \\#\\{\\text{tiles}\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a lower bound on the number of tiles is given by\n\\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(M = J-P\\).  Let \\(Q\\) be the permutation matrix that\nrealises the same permutation as \\(P\\); i.e. \\(Q_{i,p(i)}=1\\).  Then\n\n\\[\nQ^{\\!\\top} M Q = Q^{\\!\\top}(J-P)Q = J - I,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(Q^{\\!\\top}JQ = J\\) (conjugating the all‑ones matrix by a\npermutation leaves it unchanged) and \\(Q^{\\!\\top}PQ = I\\).\nSimilarity preserves rank, so\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)=\\operatorname{rank}(J-I).\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe matrix \\(J-I\\) has eigenvalues \\(n-1\\) (with eigenvector \\((1,1,\\dots ,1)^{\\!\\top}\\))\nand \\(-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  None of these eigenvalues is\nzero; therefore \\(J-I\\) is invertible and\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n.\n\\]\n\nCombining this with (4) yields\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n.\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  Consequence  \n\nFrom (3) and (5) we obtain the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{tiles}\\}\\ \\ge\\ n.\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling of the \\(n\\times n\\) board must use at least\n\\(n\\) rectangular tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nChoose any permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (for concreteness,\ntake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\)).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single tile that covers **all** squares of that\nrow except the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\).  Formally, the tile for row\n\\(i\\) occupies the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach tile is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle; different rows give disjoint\ntiles, 