                "content": "**Answer:** \\(\\displaystyle \\boxed{4048}\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.  What the uncovered squares must look like  \n\nLet the rows be numbered \\(1,2,\\dots ,2025\\) from top to bottom and the columns\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,2025\\) from left to right.\nIf a unit square \\((r,c)\\) is left uncovered, then in row \\(r\\) no other square\nis uncovered and in column \\(c\\) no other square is uncovered.\nHence the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there is a\nbijection  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,2025\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,2025\\},\n\\qquad\\text{the uncovered square in row }r\\text{ is }(r,\\sigma(r)).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  A construction with \\(2\\cdot2025-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(r)=r\\); i.e. the uncovered squares\nare the main diagonal squares \\((r,r)\\).\n\n*Left of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le 2024)\\) consider the cells\n\\[\nL_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid r>c\\}.\n\\]\nAll these cells lie strictly to the left of the diagonal, and none of them\ncontains an uncovered square.  \nThe set \\(L_c\\) is a **vertical rectangle** (its rows are the consecutive\nrows \\(c+1,c+2,\\dots ,2025\\) and its only column is \\(c\\)).\nThus we obtain \\(2024\\) tiles covering every cell left of the diagonal.\n\n*Right of the diagonal.*  \nFor each row \\(r\\;(1\\le r\\le 2024)\\) consider the cells\n\\[\nR_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid c>r\\}.\n\\]\nAll these cells lie strictly to the right of the diagonal and contain no\nuncovered square.  \nThe set \\(R_r\\) is a **horizontal rectangle** (its columns are the consecutive\ncolumns \\(r+1,r+2,\\dots ,2025\\) and its only row is \\(r\\)).\nThus we obtain another \\(2024\\) tiles covering every cell right of the\ndiagonal.\n\nThe two families are disjoint, together they cover all \\(2025^2-2025\\) squares\nthat are not on the diagonal, and the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2024+2024 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) for any \\(n\\times n\\) board  \n\nLet the board be \\(n\\times n\\) (here \\(n=2025\\)) and let the uncovered squares\nbe \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\) as above.\n\nConsider the cells **strictly left of the diagonal** of the permutation,\ni.e. the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{(r,c)\\mid c<\\sigma(r)\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each column \\(c\\) the cells of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that lie in column \\(c\\) are\nexactly the rows \\(r\\) with \\(\\sigma(r)>c\\).  \nBecause the values \\(\\sigma(r)\\) are all distinct, for a fixed \\(c\\) the set\n\\(\\{r\\mid\\sigma(r)>c\\}\\) is non‑empty for every \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\nA tile that lies completely inside \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot contain two different\ncolumns, for otherwise it would also contain a cell \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\) with\n\\(\\sigma(r)\\) equal to one of those columns – a forbidden uncovered square.\nConsequently **each column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) must contain at least one\ntile that is wholly inside \\(\\mathcal L\\)**.  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are necessary to cover \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThe same argument applied to the cells **strictly right of the diagonal**,  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{(r,c)\\mid c>\\sigma(r)\\},\n\\]\n\nshows that each row \\(r\\;(1\\le r\\le n-1)\\) must contain a distinct tile lying\nentirely inside \\(\\mathcal R\\).  Thus at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles are\nrequired.\n\nA tile cannot be simultaneously wholly inside \\(\\mathcal L\\) and wholly\ninside \\(\\mathcal R\\); therefore the total number of tiles in any tiling is at\nleast  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of Section 2 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the argument of\nSection 3 proves that no tiling can use fewer.  Hence the minimum possible\nnumber of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
