ting \\(R\\subseteq U\\). ∎\n\n   Since the \\(n-1\\) cells \\((i,i+1)\\) are all distinct, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(U\\).\n\n4. By symmetry the same argument applied to the cells \\((i+1,i)\\) in \\(L\\) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(L\\). Hence any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]  \n   tiles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Keep the uncovered squares on the main diagonal.  \n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile  \n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]  \n     which lies in \\(U\\).  \n   - For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n     \\]  \n     which lies in \\(L\\).\n\n   The \\(2n-2\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint, each tile’s sides lie on grid lines, and together they cover every cell of the board except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\). Thus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n6. Combining the lower bound and the construction, the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this number is  \n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote by \\(B\\) the set of all unit squares of the \\(n\\times n\\) grid.  \nMatilda chooses a set \\(T\\) of axis‑parallel rectangular tiles such that\n\n* each side of a tile lies on a grid line,\n* the interiors of distinct tiles are disjoint,\n* each square of \\(B\\) belongs to at most one tile.\n\nFurthermore, after the placement there must be exactly one uncovered square in each row and in each column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nLet \\(U\\subseteq B\\) be the set of uncovered squares.  \nBecause each of the \\(n\\) rows contains precisely one element of \\(U\\) and each of the \\(n\\) columns contains precisely one element of \\(U\\), the mapping\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\sigma(i)=\\text{column of the uncovered square in row }i\n\\]\nis a bijection.  Hence\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to the identity permutation\n\nIf we permute the columns of the board by the bijection \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\), the set \\(U\\) becomes the main diagonal\n\\[\n\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nA column permutation sends every rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines to another rectangle of the same type and preserves disjointness.  Consequently the minimal number of tiles needed for the original configuration equals the minimal number needed after this column relabeling.  Hence we may (and shall) assume that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Geometry of a single tile\n\nLet a tile be the rectangle\n\\[\nR=I\\times J,\\qquad\nI=\\{r_1,r_1+1,\\dots ,r_2\\},\\;J=\\{c_1,c_1+1,\\dots ,c_2\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(1\\le r_1\\le r_2\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c_1\\le c_2\\le n\\).  \nBecause \\(R\\) must avoid every diagonal square \\((i,i)\\), we cannot have simultaneously \\(i\\in I\\) and \\(i\\in J\\).  Therefore either\n\n\\[\nr_2<c_1\\qquad\\text{(the whole rectangle lies above the diagonal)},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nc_2<r_1\\qquad\\text{(the whole rectangle lies below the diagonal)}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the family of tiles splits into two disjoint subfamilies:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{A}=\\{R\\mid R\\subseteq U\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{R\\mid R\\subseteq L\\},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nU=\\{(r,c)\\mid r<c\\},\\qquad\nL=\\{(r,c)\\mid r>c\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the upper triangular region\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) cells\n\\[\nC_i=(i,i+1),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n-1,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nwhich lie immediately to the right of the diagonal.  All of them belong to \\(U\\).\n\n**Lemma 1.**  Any rectangle \\(R\\subseteq U\\) contains at most one cell of the set \\(\\{C_i\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\(R=I\\times J\\) satisfy (3).  Suppose \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells \\(C_i\\) and \\(C_j\\) with \\(i<j\\).  Then \\(i,j\\in I\\) and \\(i+1,j+1\\in J\\).  Because \\(j\\in I\\) and \\(i+1\\in J\\), the cell \\((j,i+1)\\) also belongs to \\(R\\).  Since \\(j>i+1\\), this cell lies **below** the diagonal, contradicting \\(R\\subseteq U\\).  ∎\n\nThe cells \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) are pairwise distinct, so by Lemma 1 at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover all of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A lower bound for the lower triangular region\n\nApplying the same argument to the symmetric set of cells\n\\[\nD_i=(i+1,i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n-1,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nwhich lie immediately below the diagonal, shows that any rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain at most one of the \\(D_i\\).  Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Global lower bound\n\nEvery tile belongs either to \\(\\mathcal{A}\\) or to \\(\\mathcal{B}\\).  Consequently any tiling of the whole board uses at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  A tiling attaining the bound\n\nKeep the uncovered squares on the main diagonal.  For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nwhich lies entirely in \\(U\\).  For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nwhich lies entirely in \\(L\\).\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, each has its sides on grid lines, and together they cover every cell of the board except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  Thus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (7) and the explicit construction (8)–(9) show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\#\\text{tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
