\[\n\\ell_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nExactly one index satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) and exactly one satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); therefore the families \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) each contain precisely \\(n-1\\) distinct cells.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Geometry of a tile  \n\nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; consequently it is a product of two intervals of consecutive integers:\n\\[\nR=I\\times J,\\qquad \nI=\\{a,a+1,\\dots ,b\\},\\;J=\\{c,c+1,\\dots ,d\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).\n\nSince \\(R\\) must avoid all uncovered squares \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), we have for every \\(i\\in I\\)\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)\\notin J. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 at most one right\u2011adjacent cell  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma\u202f1.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }R=I\\times J\\text{ satisfies (4), then }R\\text{ contains at most one cell }r_i.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that \\(R\\) contains two distinct right\u2011adjacent cells\n\\(r_{i}=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) and \\(r_{j}=(j,\\sigma(j)+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nBecause both columns belong to \\(J\\), we have\n\\[\nc\\le\\sigma(i)+1\\le d,\\qquad c\\le\\sigma(j)+1\\le d.\n\\]\nSince \\(J\\) is an interval, it also contains every integer between \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\).  \n\nConsider the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)+1>c\\), then \\(\\sigma(i)=\\sigma(i)+1-1\\ge c\\) and therefore \\(\\sigma(i)\\in J\\), contradicting (4).  \nHence we must have \\(\\sigma(i)+1=c\\); consequently \\(\\sigma(i)=c-1\\notin J\\).\n\nNow look at \\(\\sigma(j)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\le d\\), we have \\(\\sigma(j)=\\sigma(j)+1-1\\le d-1\\).  \nSince \\(c\\le d-1\\), the column \\(\\sigma(j)\\) belongs to the interval \\([c,d]\\), i.e. \\(\\sigma(j)\\in J\\).  \nAgain this contradicts (4).  \n\nThus a rectangle satisfying (4) cannot contain two right\u2011adjacent cells. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 at most one left\u2011adjacent cell  \n\nThe statement and proof are symmetric to Lemma\u202f1.  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma\u202f2.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }R=I\\times J\\text{ satisfies (4), then }R\\text{ contains at most one cell }\\ell_i.\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Global lower bound  \n\nThe \\(n-1\\) right\u2011adjacent cells \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) are all distinct. By Lemma\u202f1 each tile can cover at most one of them; therefore at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover all squares that lie to the right of the uncovered ones.\n\nSimilarly, the \\(n-1\\) left\u2011adjacent cells \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) are all distinct, and Lemma\u202f2 forces at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles for the squares that lie to the left of the uncovered ones.\n\nConsequently any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 \\tag{5}\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); then the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n- **Disjointness.**  \n  A horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) occupies only row \\(i\\) and columns \\(>i\\); a vertical tile \\(V_j\\) occupies only column \\(j\\) and rows \\(>j\\).  \n  If a point belonged to both a horizontal and a vertical tile, we would have a pair \\((i,j)\\) with simultaneously \\(i=j\\) (to be in the same row and column) and \\(i>j\\) (to be below the diagonal) and \\(j>i\\) (to be above the diagonal), an impossibility. Hence the tiles are pairwise disjoint.  \n\n- **Coverage.**  \n  Let \\((r,c)\\) be a non\u2011diagonal cell.  \n  If \\(r<c\\) (the cell lies above the diagonal), then \\(c\\ge r+1\\) and the horizontal tile \\(H_r\\) contains \\((r,c)\\) by definition (6).  \n  If \\(r>c\\) (the cell lies below the diagonal), then \\(r\\ge c+1\\) and the vertical tile \\(V_c\\) contains \\((r,c)\\) by definition (7).  \n  Thus every cell not on the diagonal is covered by exactly one tile.  \n\n- **Number of tiles.**  \n  The construction uses \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\nHence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nThe universal lower bound (5) and the explicit construction (6)\u2013(7) show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\#\\text{tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
