Is that minimal? Let's test small n to see if lower bound holds.\n\nFor n=2: grid 2x2, need each row and column have exactly one uncovered square. So uncovered squares must be a permutation matrix: either (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). So we have uncovered squares at diagonal or anti-diagonal. The rest squares: if diagonal uncovered, then uncovered squares are (1,1) and (2,2). The other squares are (1,2) and (2,1). Can we cover them with a single tile? A tile must be a rectangle that does not include any uncovered squares. The squares (1,2) and (2,1) are not adjacent in a rectangle shape: the rectangle covering them would be the whole 2x2 board, which includes uncovered squares. So we need two tiles, each covering one of those squares. So minimum tiles = 2. The formula 2n-2 = 2*2-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nFor n=3: need uncovered squares at positions (i, sigma(i)). Let's try sigma = identity (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Then uncovered squares are diagonal. The rest squares are those above diagonal (positions (i,j) with j>i) and below diagonal (i>j). The solution uses horizontal tiles for each row i covering columns i+1..n (i.e., all squares to the right of diagonal in that row) and vertical tiles for each column j covering rows j+1..n (i.e., all squares below diagonal in that column). That yields (n-1)+(n-1) = 4 tiles for n=3: horizontal tiles: H1 covers (1,2),(1,3); H2 covers (2,3). That's 2 horizontal tiles. Vertical tiles: V1 covers (2,1),(3,1); V2 covers (3,2). That's 2 vertical tiles. So total 4 tiles. The formula gives 2*3-2 = 4. Is it minimal? Could we do fewer? Let's see if we can cover all 6 squares (9 total minus 3 uncovered = 6 squares) with 3 rectangles? Possibly. For n=3, we need to cover 6 squares. Each rectangle must avoid diagonal squares. Could we have a rectangle covering (1,2),(1,3),(2,3) i.e., shape L? Not a rectangle. Could we have a rectangle covering (1,2),(2,2),(2,3) maybe? That includes (2,2) which is uncovered. So not allowed. Could we have a rectangle covering (1,2),(2,2)? No. So maybe we need at least 4 rectangles. Let's think systematically: The uncovered squares are at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). The squares above diagonal are (1,2),(1,3),(2,3). Those three squares are not in a single rectangle because they are not contiguous in a rectangle shape: they form a shape like a 2x2 block missing (2,2). The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover that shape (the upper triangle) is 2: one rectangle covering (1,2),(1,3) (row 1, columns 2-3) and one rectangle covering (2,3) (single cell). Similarly for lower triangle: (2,1),(3,1),(3,2) can be covered by two rectangles: vertical tile covering (2,1),(3,1) and single cell (3,2). So total 4 rectangles. So minimal is 4. So formula holds.\n\nFor n=4: formula gives 6 tiles. Let's test if we can do less. The uncovered squares are diagonal. The shape of squares above diagonal is a triangular region of size (n choose 2) = 6 squares. The shape is a Young diagram: rows of lengths n-1, n-2, ..., 1. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram into rectangles (axis-aligned) is known to be n-1 (the number of rows) if you use horizontal strips each covering a row's right part. But you could also use vertical strips each covering a column's top part. But you cannot cover the whole triangle with fewer than n-1 rectangles? Let's think: The shape is a Ferrers diagram of partition (n-1,n-2,...,1). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Ferrers diagram into rectangles is equal to the number of rows (or columns) whichever is smaller? Actually there is a known result: The rectangle covering number (also called the \"rectangular partition number\") for a Ferrers diagram is the size of the largest antichain in the poset of cells under the partial order (i.e., the size of the largest \"staircase\"?). But maybe the minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the upper triangle is n-1. Indeed the solution uses n-1 horizontal rectangles. Could we use fewer? Could we combine some rows? For example, for n=4, the upper triangle cells are:\n\nRow1: columns 2,3,4 (3 cells)\nRow2: columns 3,4 (2 cells)\nRow3: column 4 (1 cell)\n\nWe could cover them with 3 rectangles: H1 covering row1 columns 2-4 (3 cells), H2 covering row2 columns 3-4 (2 cells), H3 covering row3 column 4 (1 cell). That's 3 rectangles = n-1 = 3. So lower bound for upper triangle is n-1. Similarly lower triangle also needs n-1 rectangles. So total 2n-2 = 6. Could we combine some rectangles across the diagonal? No, because any rectangle that contains cells from both above and below diagonal would cross the diagonal and include a diagonal cell (uncovered). So you cannot have a rectangle that includes both a cell above and a cell below the diagonal. So the covering of the upper triangle and lower triangle must be separate. So total minimal is 2n-2.\n\nThus answer seems correct.\n\nNow we need to evaluate the solution's reasoning. The solution uses lemmas about right-adjacent and left-adjacent cells. The lemmas claim each tile can contain at most one right-adjacent cell (or left-adjacent). The proof uses intervals and contradictions. We need to check if the lemmas are valid. Let's examine them.\n\nDefine uncovered squares U = {(i, sigma(i))}. For each row i, define right-adjacent cell r_i = (i, sigma(i)+1) if sigma(i) < n. Similarly left-adjacent cell l_i = (i, sigma(i)-1) if sigma(i) > 1.\n\nThus there are n-1 right-adjacent cells (since exactly one sigma(i) = n, so that row has no right-adjacent cell). Similarly n-1 left-adjacent cells.\n\nLemma 1: If a rectangle R = I x J avoids all uncovered squares (i.e., for each i in I, sigma(i) not in J), then R contains at most one right-adjacent cell.\n\nProof: Suppose R contains two right-adjacent cells r_i and r_j. Then sigma(i)+1 and sigma(j)+1 are both in J. Since J is an interval, all columns between them are also in J. Then they argue that sigma(i) must be in J (contradiction). Let's examine the proof.\n\nAssume i<j