ers both a right-adjacent cell and a left-adjacent cell? If so, the lower bound could be less than 2n-2. Let's examine.\n\nGiven a tile R = I x J that avoids uncovered squares. It can contain at most one right-adjacent cell and at most one left-adjacent cell. Could it contain both? Possibly if there are rows i and k such that r_i = (i, sigma(i)+1) and l_k = (k, sigma(k)-1) are both in R. That would mean sigma(i)+1 in J and sigma(k)-1 in J. Since J is an interval, it would contain all columns between sigma(i)+1 and sigma(k)-1. But we need to ensure that sigma(i) is not in J and sigma(k) is not in J. Let's see if this is possible.\n\nSuppose sigma(i) < sigma(k). Then sigma(i)+1 <= sigma(k)-1 (if sigma(i)+1 <= sigma(k)-1). Then J includes sigma(i)+1 and sigma(k)-1, so includes all columns between them, including sigma(i)+1,...,sigma(k)-1. In particular, sigma(i)+1 is in J, sigma(k)-1 is in J. Does this force sigma(i) or sigma(k) to be in J? Possibly not. For sigma(i), sigma(i) = (sigma(i)+1)-1. If sigma(i)+1 > c (the left endpoint of J), then sigma(i) >= c, so sigma(i) could be in J. But if sigma(i)+1 = c, then sigma(i) = c-1 not in J. Similarly for sigma(k), sigma(k) = (sigma(k)-1)+1. If sigma(k)-1 < d (right endpoint), then sigma(k) <= d, so sigma(k) could be in J. But if sigma(k)-1 = d, then sigma(k) = d+1 not in J. So to avoid sigma(i) and sigma(k) being in J, we need sigma(i)+1 = c (i.e., sigma(i) = c-1) and sigma(k)-1 = d (i.e., sigma(k) = d+1). Then J = [c, d] = [sigma(i)+1, sigma(k)-1]. So J is exactly the interval between sigma(i)+1 and sigma(k)-1 inclusive. Then sigma(i) = c-1 not in J, sigma(k) = d+1 not in J. So it's possible for a rectangle to contain both a right-adjacent cell and a left-adjacent cell, provided those are at the extremes of J. But does such a rectangle also avoid uncovered squares for all rows in I? Condition (4) requires sigma(i) not in J for all i in I. So if I contains rows i and k, we need sigma(i) not in J and sigma(k) not in J. That's satisfied as above. But also for any other row i' in I, sigma(i') must not be in J. So the rectangle can contain at most one right-adjacent cell and at most one left-adjacent cell, but could contain both. So the lower bound of 2n-2 might be too high if we can combine a right-adjacent cell and a left-adjacent cell in the same tile, reducing total needed.\n\nBut the solution's lower bound argument says: \"The n-1 right-adjacent cells are all distinct. By Lemma 1 each tile can cover at most one of them; therefore at least n-1 tiles are required to cover all squares that lie to the right of the uncovered ones. Similarly, ... at least another n-1 tiles for the squares that lie to the left.\" This reasoning implicitly assumes that tiles covering right-adjacent cells cannot also cover left-adjacent cells, i.e., that the sets of tiles covering right-adjacent cells and left-adjacent cells are disjoint. However, they didn't prove that a tile covering a right-adjacent cell cannot also cover a left-adjacent cell. Lemma 1 only says at most one right-adjacent cell per tile; Lemma 2 says at most one left-adjacent cell per tile. But they could be the same tile covering both a right-adjacent cell and a left-adjacent cell. So the lower bound argument is flawed unless they prove that a tile cannot contain both a right- and a left-adjacent cell. Did they prove that? No. They didn't. So there is a potential gap: they might be overcounting.\n\nBut perhaps there is a reason why a tile cannot contain both a right- and left-adjacent cell because of the structure of the permutation matrix. Let's examine more carefully. Right-adjacent cells are to the right of uncovered squares; left-adjacent cells are to the left of uncovered squares. For a given row i, the right-adjacent cell is at column sigma(i)+1 (if sigma(i) < n). The left-adjacent cell is at column sigma(i)-1 (if sigma(i) > 1). So for a given row, there could be both a right- and left-adjacent cell (if sigma(i) is not at extreme). But those are in the same row. However, a tile is a rectangle that includes a set of rows I and a set of columns J. If a tile contains both a right-adjacent cell (i, sigma(i)+1) and a left-adjacent cell (i, sigma(i)-1) in the same row i, then J must contain both sigma(i)-1 and sigma(i)+1, thus also sigma(i) (since J is an interval). But sigma(i) is uncovered, so tile would contain uncovered square, which is not allowed. So a tile cannot contain both right- and left-adjacent cells from the same row. But could a tile contain a right-adjacent cell from row i and a left-adjacent cell from a different row k? Possibly. Let's examine.\n\nSuppose tile contains r_i = (i, sigma(i)+1) and l_k = (k, sigma(k)-1). Then J must contain sigma(i)+1 and sigma(k)-1. As before, J is an interval, so it contains all columns between them. If sigma(i)+1 <= sigma(k)-1, then J contains sigma(i)+1,...,sigma(k)-1. Does this force sigma(i) or sigma(k) to be in J? Possibly. But we need to check both possibilities.\n\nCase 1: sigma(i)+1 <= sigma(k)-1. Then J includes sigma(i)+1 and sigma(k)-1. Since J is an interval, it includes sigma(i)+1,...,sigma(k)-1. Then sigma(i) = sigma(i)+1 - 1. If sigma(i)+1 > c (left endpoint), then sigma(i) >= c, so sigma(i) is in J. But if sigma(i)+1 = c, then sigma(i) = c-1 not in J. Similarly, sigma(k) = sigma(k)-1 + 1. If sigma(k)-1 < d (right endpoint), then sigma(k) <= d, so sigma(k) in J. But if sigma(k)-1 = d, then sigma(k) = d+1 not in J. So for both sigma(i) and sigma(k) to be outside J, we need sigma(i)+1 = c and sigma(k)-1 = d. So J = [sigma(i)+1, sigma(k)-1] exactly. This is possible if sigma(i)+1 <= sigma(k)-1. So we need sigma(i)+1 <= sigma(k)-1, i.e., sigma(i)+2 <= sigma(k). So sigma(k) >= sigma(i)+2.\n\nThus we can have a rectangle that includes right-adjacent cell of row i and left-adjacent cell of row k, provided sigma(k) >= sigma(i)+2 and the rectangle's column interval is exactly from sigma(i)+1 to sigma(k)-1. Then sigma(i) and sigma(k) are not in J. However, we also need to ensure that for any other row i' in I, sigma(i') not in J. So the rows set I must be such that for each i' in I, sigma(i') not in the interval [sigma(i)+1, sigma(k)-1]. That means sigma(i') must be either <= sigma(i) or >= sigma(k). So the rows in I must have sigma-values outside that interval. This is possible if the permutation sigma has some structure.\n\nThus it's possible for a tile to contain both a right-adjacent cell and a left-adjacent cell, as long as the rows are chosen appropriately. So the lower bound argument that adds n-1 + n-1 may be too high. However, the solution's construction uses exactly 2n-2 tiles, so the lower bound is at most that. But is there a possibility to achieve fewer than 2n-2 tiles? Let's explore.\n\nWe need to cover all squares except the n uncovered ones. The uncovered squares are a permutation matrix. The complement consists of all squares not on the permutation. This is essentially the board minus a permutation matrix. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is known as the \"rectangular covering number\" of the complement of a permutation matrix. This is related to the \"grid number\" or \"grid covering number\" of permutations. I recall a known result: The minimal number of axis-aligned rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is exactly 2n - 2. This is known as the \"grid number\" of a permutation (the number of monotone subsequences needed to cover the permutation?). Actually, there is a known combinatorial result: For any permutation of [n], the complement of its graph (the set of points (i, sigma(i))) can be partitioned into at most 2n - 2 monotone rectangles (or \"grid lines\"?). This is known as the \"grid number\" of a permutation, which is at most 2n - 2, and this bound is tight for the identity permutation, requiring exactly 2n - 2 rectangles. Indeed, the identity permutation's complement is the union of the upper 