J. However, we also need to ensure that for any other row i' in I, sigma(i') not in J. So the rows set I must be such that for each i' in I, sigma(i') not in the interval [sigma(i)+1, sigma(k)-1]. That means sigma(i') must be either <= sigma(i) or >= sigma(k). So the rows in I must have sigma-values outside that interval. This is possible if the permutation sigma has some structure.\n\nThus it's possible for a tile to contain both a right-adjacent cell and a left-adjacent cell, as long as the rows are chosen appropriately. So the lower bound argument that adds n-1 + n-1 may be too high. However, the solution's construction uses exactly 2n-2 tiles, so the lower bound is at most that. But is there a possibility to achieve fewer than 2n-2 tiles? Let's explore.\n\nWe need to cover all squares except the n uncovered ones. The uncovered squares are a permutation matrix. The complement consists of all squares not on the permutation. This is essentially the board minus a permutation matrix. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is known as the \"rectangular covering number\" of the complement of a permutation matrix. This is related to the \"grid number\" or \"grid covering number\" of permutations. I recall a known result: The minimal number of axis-aligned rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is exactly 2n - 2. This is known as the \"grid number\" of a permutation (the number of monotone subsequences needed to cover the permutation?). Actually, there is a known combinatorial result: For any permutation of [n], the complement of its graph (the set of points (i, sigma(i))) can be partitioned into at most 2n - 2 monotone rectangles (or \"grid lines\"?). This is known as the \"grid number\" of a permutation, which is at most 2n - 2, and this bound is tight for the identity permutation, requiring exactly 2n - 2 rectangles. Indeed, the identity permutation's complement is the union of the upp