\).  Set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{A_i,B_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|S|=2(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma  \n*No axis‑parallel rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares can contain two\ndistinct elements of \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(R\\times C\\) be a rectangle with \\(R=[r_1,r_2]\\) and \\(C=[c_1,c_2]\\) (both\nintervals of integers) such that \\((R\\times C)\\cap U=\\varnothing\\).\nAssume that it contains two distinct points of \\(S\\).  According to (2) there are\nthree possibilities.\n\n1. **Both points are of type \\(A\\):**  \n   Suppose \\(A_i=(i,\\pi(i+1))\\) and \\(A_j=(j,\\pi(j+1))\\) with \\(i<j\\).\n   Then \\(i,j\\in R\\) and \\(\\pi(i+1),\\pi(j+1)\\in C\\).  \n   Consequently every integer between \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) and \\(\\pi(j+1)\\) also lies in\n   \\(C\\).  For each \\(k\\) with \\(i<k\\le j\\) the hole \\((k,\\pi(k))\\) satisfies\n   \\(\\pi(k)\\in\\{\\pi(i+1),\\dots ,\\pi(j)\\}\\subseteq C\\) and \\(k\\in R\\); hence\n   \\((k,\\pi(k))\\in R\\times C\\), contradicting the assumption that the rectangle\n   avoids all holes.\n\n2. **Both points are of type \\(B\\):**  \n   Suppose \\(B_i=(i+1,\\pi(i))\\) and \\(B_j=(j+1,\\pi(j))\\) with \\(i<j\\).\n   Then \\(i+1,j+1\\in R\\) and \\(\\pi(i),\\pi(j)\\in C\\).  \n   As before every integer between \\(\\pi(i)\\) and \\(\\pi(j)\\) belongs to \\(C\\).\n   For each \\(k\\) with \\(i+1\\le k\\le j\\) we have \\((k,\\pi(k))\\in R\\times C\\),\n   again a forbidden hole.\n\n3. **One point of type \\(A\\) and one of type \\(B\\):**  \n   *If they correspond to the same index* \\(i\\), the two points are\n   \\(A_i=(i,\\pi(i+1))\\) and \\(B_i=(i+1,\\pi(i))\\).  \n   The rectangle must contain rows \\(i,i+1\\) and columns \\(\\pi(i),\\pi(i+1)\\);\n   hence it also contains the two holes \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) and \\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\).\n\n   *If they correspond to different indices* \\(i<j\\), the rectangle contains\n   rows \\(i\\) and \\(j+1\\) and columns \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) and \\(\\pi(j)\\).\n   As in the previous cases, every integer between \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) and\n   \\(\\pi(j)\\) lies in \\(C\\); for each \\(k\\) with \\(i<k\\le j\\) the hole\n   \\((k,\\pi(k))\\) belongs to the rectangle, a contradiction.\n\nIn every possible situation a hole would be forced to lie inside the rectangle,\ncontrary to the definition of a tile.  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom the lemma, each tile can cover **at most one** element of the set \\(S\\).\nSince \\(|S|=2n-2\\), any tiling of \\(R\\) must contain at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge 2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nInequalities (1) and (2) together give\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor the problem’s specific size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence Matilda cannot do better than placing **4048** tiles, and this number is\nattainable by the construction of Section 1.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
