utation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\},\n\\]\n\nand for each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{down}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(T^{\\text{up}}_j\\) (resp. \\(T^{\\text{down}}_j\\)) is a rectangle of width\u202f1\nand height \\(j-1\\) (resp. \\(n-j\\)), hence its sides lie on grid lines.\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\{T^{\\text{up}}_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\cup\n          \\{T^{\\text{down}}_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) exactly once:\nif \\(i<j\\) the square belongs to \\(T^{\\text{up}}_j\\); if \\(i>j\\) it belongs to\n\\(T^{\\text{down}}_j\\).  The diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) are uncovered, giving one\nuncovered square in each row and each column.\n\nThe number of non\u2011empty tiles in \\(\\mathcal T\\) is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus a placement with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (2) shows that no placement can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles,\nand construction (3) shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
