ast 2n-2 tiles. Then they construct a tiling achieving exactly 2n-2 tiles: for identity permutation, they use vertical tiles: for each column j, they have an \"up\" tile covering squares above the diagonal (i<j) and a \"down\" tile covering squares below the diagonal (i>j). Actually they define T_up_j = {(i,j) | 1 ≤ i < j} for j = 2..n, and T_down_j = {(i,j) | j < i ≤ n} for j = 1..n-1. So each column j has two tiles: one covering squares above the diagonal, one covering squares below. However note that for column j, the squares above the diagonal are those with row i < j (i.e., rows 1..j-1). The squares below the diagonal are those with row i > j (i.e., rows j+1..n). So each column j has two tiles, except maybe column 1 and column n? Let's check: For j = 1, T_up_1 is not defined (since j starts at 2). So column 1 only has T_down_1 = {(i,1) | 1 < i ≤ n} = rows 2..n. That's one tile. For j = n, T_down_n is not defined (since j up to n-1). So column n only has T_up_n = {(i,n) | 1 ≤ i < n} = rows 1..n-1. That's one tile. So total number of tiles: For j = 2..n-1, we have both up and down tiles, giving 2*(n-2) tiles. Plus column 1 down tile (1) and column n up tile (1). So total = 2*(n-2) + 2 = 2n-2. Yes.\n\nThus the construction uses exactly 2n-2 tiles. So the answer seems correct.\n\nBut we must verify if the lower bound is rigorous. The solution defines S = {A_i, B_i} for i = 1..n-1, where A_i = (i, π(i+1)), B_i = (i+1, π(i)). They claim that any tile that avoids all uncovered squares (i.e., any rectangle not intersecting U) can contain at most one point of S. They prove that if a rectangle contains two points of S, then it must contain a hole (i.e., uncovered square) from U, which is a contradiction. The proof seems correct.\n\nThus the lower bound of at least |S| = 2n-2 tiles is valid. Then the construction shows that 2n-2 tiles are sufficient. So the answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to check if any reasoning uses invalid methods. The solution uses case analysis, c