es \\((i,j)\\) with \\(p(i)<j\\le n\\); its length is  \n  \\[\n    r_i:=n-p(i).\n  \\]\n\nSince \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); consequently the set of positive left lengths is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThe same holds for the right lengths \\(\\{r_i\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   A lower‑bound lemma for rectangle partitions  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\n\\]\nLet \\(S\\) be a finite set of unit squares such that for each row \\(i\\) the squares of \\(S\\) in that row form a contiguous segment of length \\(a_i\\ge0\\).  \nAssume the set \\(\\{a_i>0\\}\\) contains \\(m\\) distinct values.  \nThen any partition of \\(S\\) into axis‑aligned rectangles uses at least \\(m\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each distinct positive value \\(t\\) choose the unique row \\(i_t\\) with \\(a_{i_t}=t\\).  \nIn row \\(i_t\\) the rightmost square of \\(S\\) is \\((i_t,t)\\).  \n\nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\) of a partition that contains \\((i_t,t)\\).  \nIf the rightmost column of \\(R\\) were larger than \\(t\\), then \\(R\\) would also contain the square \\((i_t,t+1)\\), which does **not** belong to \\(S\\) (row \\(i_t\\) has no squares beyond column \\(t\\)).  \nHence the rightmost column of \\(R\\) must be exactly \\(t\\).  \n\nTwo different values \\(t\\neq t'\\) force the rightmost columns of the rectangles covering \\((i_t,t)\\) and \\((i_{t'},t')\\) to be different, so the two squares belong to distinct rectangles.  \nThus at least one rectangle is needed for each of the \\(m\\) distinct values, proving the lemma. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Applying the lemma to the left and right sides  \n\nDefine the **left region**\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nIn row \\(i\\) the region \\(L\\) occupies exactly \\(\\ell_i=p(i)-1\\) squares, so the row‑lengths of \\(L\\) are \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\).  \nThe set of positive left lengths is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); therefore it contains \\(n-1\\) distinct values.  \nBy the Lemma, any rectangle partition of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nSimilarly, the **right region**\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}\n\\]\nhas row‑lengths \\(r_i=n-p(i)\\).  \nThe positive right lengths are also \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), so any partition of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nSince every tile of a valid tiling must lie entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\) (otherwise it would contain a hole), the total number of tiles in any tiling is at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_{k}:=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T^{\\text{L}}_{k}\\) is a \\((n-k)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \nBecause the hole in row \\(i\\) lies at column \\(i\\ge k+1\\), every square of \\(T^{\\text{L}}_{k}\\) is to the left of the hole in its row; thus all its squares are covered.\n\n*Right tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define\n\\[\nT^{\\text{R}}_{k}:=\\{(k,j)\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T^{\\text{R}}_{k}\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-k)\\) rectangle, and each of its squares lies to the right of the hole \\((k,k)\\) in its row, so they are covered.\n\n*Disjointness and completeness.*  \nTwo distinct left tiles occupy different columns, hence are disjoint.  \nTwo distinct right tiles occupy different rows, hence are disjoint.  \nA left tile \\(T^{\\text{L}}_{k}\\) and a right tile \\(T^{\\text{R}}_{\\ell}\\) could intersect only at the cell \\((\\ell,k)\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell=k\\) this cell is the diagonal