e the unique row \\(i_t\\) with \\(\\ell_{i_t}=t\\).  \n   The rightmost square of \\(L\\) in row \\(i_t\\) is \\((i_t,t)\\).  \n   Any rectangle covering \\((i_t,t)\\) must have its rightmost column equal to \\(t\\); otherwise it would also contain column \\(t+1\\) in row \\(i_t\\), which does not belong to \\(L\\).  \n   Distinct values of \\(t\\) force distinct rightmost columns, hence distinct rectangles. ∎  \n\n3. **Lower bound.**  \n   In the left region \\(L\\) the positive lengths are \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus Lemma 2 gives at least \\(n-1\\) left‑type rectangles.  \n   By symmetry the right region\n   \\[\n   R=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n   also forces at least \\(n-1\\) right‑type rectangles.  \n   Lemma 1 guarantees that every tile is either left‑type or right‑type, never both.  \n   Consequently any tiling uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     T^{\\text{L}}_{k}=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\((n-k)\\times1\\) rectangle covering all squares left of the diagonal in column \\(k\\).  \n   - For each row \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal tile  \n     \\[\n     T^{\\text{R}}_{k}=\\{(k,j)\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\(1\\times(n-k)\\) rectangle covering all squares right of the diagonal in row \\(k\\).\n\n   The \\(T^{\\text{L}}_{k}\\) are pairwise disjoint (different columns), the \\(T^{\\text{R}}_{k}\\) are pairwise disjoint (different rows), and a left tile never meets a right tile because any possible intersection would be a diagonal square \\((k,k)\\), which is uncovered.  \n   Thus the \\(2n-2\\) tiles cover every unit square except the diagonal squares, satisfying the required condition.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   