,c_2]\\) be a set of consecutive columns.  \nAssume the rectangle \\(R=I\\times C\\) contains none of the holes \\((i,p(i))\\) for \\(i\\in I\\).  \nThen either  \n\\[\nC\\subseteq[1,p(i)-1]\\quad\\text{for every }i\\in I\n\\]\nor  \n\\[\nC\\subseteq[p(i)+1,n]\\quad\\text{for every }i\\in I.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose the contrary. Then there exist rows \\(i,j\\in I\\) such that  \n\\(C\\cap[1,p(i)-1]\\neq\\varnothing\\) (so \\(C\\) contains a column \\(a<p(i)\\)) and  \n\\(C\\cap[p(j)+1,n]\\neq\\varnothing\\) (so \\(C\\) contains a column \\(b>p(j)\\)).  \nBecause \\(C\\) is an interval, it contains every column between \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).  \n\nIf \\(p(i)\\le p(j)\\) then \\(p(i)\\in[a,b]\\); if \\(p(i)>p(j)\\) then \\(p(j)\\in[a,b]\\).  \nThus \\(C\\) contains a column equal to \\(p(k)\\) for some \\(k\\in\\{i,j\\}\\), which means the rectangle would contain the hole \\((k,p(k))\\), contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\nConsequences of Lemma 1: every tile of a valid tiling is **entirely** contained in \\(L\\) (a *left‑type* tile) or entirely in \\(R\\) (a *right‑type* tile).  No tile can contain squares from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 2 – distinct left lengths need distinct left tiles  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\n\\]  \nLet \\(L\\) be as defined above.  If the set \\(\\{\\ell_i>0\\}\\) contains \\(m\\) distinct values, then any partition of \\(L\\) into axis‑aligned rectangles uses at least \\(m\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each distinct positive value \\(t\\) choose the unique row \\(i_t\\) with \\(\\ell_{i_t}=t\\).  \nThe rightmost square of \\(L\\) in that row is \\((i_t,t)\\).  \n\nConsider any rectangle \\(Q\\) of a partition that contains \\((i_t,t)\\).  \nIf the rightmost column of \\(Q\\) were larger than \\(t\\), then \\(Q\\) would also contain the square \\((i_t,t+1)\\), which does **not** belong to \\(L\\).  \nHence the rightmost column of \\(Q\\) must be exactly