ext{R}}_{\\ell}\\) consists of squares with row \\(\\ell\\) and columns \\(>\\ell\\).  \nIf \\(k\\neq\\ell\\) the two sets of coordinates are different; if \\(k=\\ell\\) their only possible common square would be \\((k,k)\\), which is a diagonal (uncovered) square and therefore belongs to neither tile.  \nHence the family \\(\\{T^{\\text{L}}_{k}\\}\\cup\\{T^{\\text{R}}_{k}\\}\\) consists of \\(2(n-1)\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles covering every square of the board except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\nThus we have a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Optimality  \n\nEquation (2) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
