e sides lie on grid lines; it therefore consists of a set  \n\\[\nT=\\{(r,c): a\\le r\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\le d\\}\n\\]\nfor some integers \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Geometry of admissible tiles  \n\nEach row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares forms a **permutation matrix**: there exists a bijection\n\\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i)) : 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a tile contained two squares \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) and \\((j,\\sigma(j))\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), then it would also contain the square \\((i,\\sigma(j))\\) (or \\((j,\\sigma(i))\\)), contradicting the requirement that uncovered squares are the only squares left empty.  \nHence **no tile may contain any uncovered square**.  \n\nConsider a tile \\(R\\) that lies in the region above the main diagonal, i.e. all its squares satisfy \\(r<c\\).  \nWrite its row interval as \\([a,b]\\) and its column interval as \\([c,d]\\).  \nIf \\(R\\) contained a square with \\(r=c\\) (i.e. a diagonal square), then \\((c,c)\\) would be covered, which is forbidden.  \nTherefore we must have\n\\[\nb<c. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThus any tile situated above the diagonal satisfies (1); analogously any tile situated below the diagonal satisfies\n\\[\nd<a. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every tile belongs **entirely** to the set\n\\[\nH=\\{(r,c): r<c\\}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad L=\\{(r,c): r>c\\},\n\\]\nthe two open half‑boards determined by the diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the upper half‑board  \n\nDefine the set of “adjacent‑diagonal’’ squares\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,i+1):1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\subseteq H .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{A rectangle satisfying (1) can contain at most one element of }S.\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet a rectangle \\(R\\) satisfy (1) and suppose it contains two distinct squares\n\\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nBecause the row interval of \\(R\\) is contiguous, it must contain all rows from \\(i\\) up to \\(j\\); hence its maximal row index \\(b\\) satisfies \\(b\\ge j\\).  \nSimilarly its column interval must contain both columns \\(i+1\\) and \\(j+1\\); therefore its minimal column index \\(c\\) satisfies \\(c\\le i+1\\).  \nSince \\(j\\ge i+1\\), we have \\(b\\ge j\\ge i+1\\ge c\\), contradicting the required inequality \\(b<c\\). ∎\n\nThe squares of \\(S\\) are pairwise distinct, and each must be covered by some tile lying in \\(H\\).  \nBy the claim, each such tile can cover at most one element of \\(S\\); therefore at least \\(|S|=n-1\\) tiles are needed to cover all of \\(H\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the lower half‑board  \n\nBy symmetry, consider the set\n\\[\nT=\\{(i,i-1):2\\le i\\le n\\}\\subseteq L .\n\\]\nAn argument identical to the one above shows that any rectangle satisfying (2) can contain at most one element of \\(T\\).  \nHence at least \\(|T|=n-1\\) tiles are required to cover the lower half‑board \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Global lower bound  \n\nEvery admissible tiling must use at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.  \nFor \\(n=2025\\) this yields the bound \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); i.e., leave the main diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) uncovered.  \nNow place the following tiles:\n\n*For the upper half‑board.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) put a horizontal tile\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,c):i+1\\le c\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.  \nThese \\(n-