 \nR=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of covered squares is \\(L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<i\\},\\qquad R=\\{(i,j):j>i\\},\n\\]\n\nthe two triangular regions determined by the main diagonal.\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{k}=\\{(i,j):k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal{L}_{k}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times k\\).  \nThe sets \\(\\mathcal{L}_{1},\\dots ,\\mathcal{L}_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is exactly \\(L\\).  \nHence \\(L\\) is tiled by \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{R}_{k}=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na rectangle of size \\(k\\times (n-k)\\).  \nThe sets \\(\\mathcal{R}_{1},\\dots ,\\mathcal{R}_{n-1}\\) are disjoint and their union is \\(R\\).  \nThus \\(R\\) is tiled by another \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nAltogether we obtain a tiling of all covered squares with  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangular tiles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(4048\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nWe prove that no placement of the uncovered squares can be tiled with fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1.  The left region \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\sigma\\) be a permutation of the rows such that  \n\n\\[\np(\\sigma(1))<p(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <p(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\n\nReorder the rows of the board according to \\(\\sigma\\); the set of squares belonging to \\(L\\) is unchanged, only the order