condition “\\(j>p(i)\\)” is equivalent to “\\(n+1-j<q(i)\\)”.  Hence the set \\(R\\) is the left region for the permutation \\(q\\).  Applying Lemma 1 to \\(q\\) yields the desired bound for \\(R\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Completion of the lower bound  \n\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); the two sets are disjoint.  \nConsequently any tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{rectangles covering }L}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{rectangles covering }R}\n\\;=\\;2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of Section 1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and Lemma 1 together with Lemma 2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
