"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Encoding the uncovered squares.**  \n   - Let \\(n=2025\\).  \n   - In each row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) the unique uncovered unit square lies in some column \\(p(i)\\).  \n   - Because each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a bijection; i.e. \\(p\\) is a permutation.\n\n2. **Splitting the covered region.**  \n   - Define the *left* region  \n     \\[\n     L=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n     \\]\n     and the *right* region  \n     \\[\n     R=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n     \\]  \n   - The set of covered squares is exactly \\(L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n3. **Lower bound for each region.**  \n   - **Lemma 1 (left region).** The set  \n     \\[\n     S_L=\\{(i,p(i)-1):p(i)>1\\}\n     \\]\n     consists of \\(n-1\\) cells, each in a distinct row and a distinct column.  \n     No axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain two distinct cells of \\(S_L\\); otherwise it would also contain a cell \\((i_1,p(i_2)-1)\\) that does not satisfy the condition \\(j<p(i_1)\\).  \n     Hence any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(|S_L|=n-1\\) rectangles.  \n   - **Lemma 2 (right region).** By symmetry, the set  \n     \\[\n     S_R=\\{(i,p(i)+1):p(i)<n\\}\n     \\]\n     also has \\(n-1\\) cells in distinct rows and columns, and no rectangle inside \\(R\\) can contain two of them.  \n     Therefore any tiling of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n   - Consequently any tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n     \\[\n     (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n     \\]\n     rectangles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   - Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\). Then the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - **Tiling \\(L\\).** For each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) place a rectangle covering the cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots,(i,i-1)\\). This is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle. The \\(n-1\\) such rectangles are disjoint and together cover all of \\(L\\).  \n   - **Tiling \\(R\\).** For each column \\(j\\) with \\(j\\ge2\\) place a rectangle covering the cells \\((1,j),(2,j),\\dots,(j-1,j)\\). This is a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle. The \\(n-1\\) such rectangles are disjoint and together cover all of \\(R\\).  \n   - The total number of tiles used is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.** The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimal possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For the given grid,\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nis a bijection; thus \\(p\\) is a permutation.\n\nDefine\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nEvery covered unit square belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\), and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Lower bound for the left region\n\nConsider the set\n\\[\nS_L=\\{(i,p(i)-1):p(i)>1\\}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(p(i)-1\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) are precisely the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\), each occurring exactly once. Hence\n\\[\n|S_L|=n-1,\n\\]\nand the cells of \\(S_L\\) lie in distinct rows and distinct columns.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{No axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained in }L\\text{ can contain two distinct cells of }S_L.\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) contains two distinct cells \\((i_1,c_1)\\) and \\((i_2,c_2)\\) of \\(S_L\\), where without loss of generality \\(i_1<i_2\\). By definition,\n\\[\nc_1=p(i_1)-1,\\qquad c_2=p(i_2)-1.\n\\]\nSince \\(Q\\) is a rectangle, its leftmost column is at most its rightmost column, so \\(c_1\\le c_2\\). Consequently \\(p(i_1)\\le p(i_2)\\). The rectangle \\(Q\\) then also contains the cell \\((i_1,c_2)\\). Because \\(c_2\\ge p(i_1)\\) (indeed \\(c_2=p(i_2)-1\\ge p(i_1)\\)), this cell does **not** satisfy the condition \\(j<p(i_1)\\) required for membership in \\(L\\). Hence \\((i_1,c_2)\\notin L\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(Q\\subseteq L\\). \u220e\n\nThus each rectangle covering a part of \\(L\\) can contain at most one element of \\(S_L\\). Since all elements of \\(S_L\\) must be covered, any tiling of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(|S_L|=n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound for the right region\n\nDefine\n\\[\nS_R=\\{(i,p(i)+1):p(i)<n\\}.\n\\]\nAgain, because \\(p\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(p(i)+1\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) are exactly the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\), each appearing once; therefore \\(|S_R|=n-1\\). The cells of \\(S_R\\) lie in distinct rows and distinct columns, and each belongs to the right part of its row, i.e. to \\(R\\).\n\nThe same argument as in the claim for \\(L\\) (applied symmetrically to the set \\(R\\)) shows that no rectangle contained in \\(R\\) can contain two distinct cells of \\(S_R\\). Consequently any tiling of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nSince \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, a tiling of the whole board must contain at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction achieving the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\nThen the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \nConsequently\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<i\\},\\qquad R=\\{(i,j):j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Tiling \\(L\\).**  \nFor each row index \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place a rectangle\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle. The rectangles \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union equals \\(L\\). Hence \\(L\\) is tiled by exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n**Tiling \\(R\\).**  \nFor each column index \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a rectangle\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\\(V_j\\) is a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle. The rectangles \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union equals \\(R\\). Thus \\(R\\) is tiled by exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nTogether the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\cup\\{V_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) cover all squares except the diagonal cells, each of which is uncovered exactly once per row and per column. Therefore this is a valid placement of tiles using \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Any tiling must use at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles (lower bound).\n* There exists a tiling using exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles (construction).\n\nHence the minimal possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given grid with \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n* The summary states the final answer in \\boxed{} format and outlines the method correctly.\n* The detailed solution contains a complete, gap\u2011free proof of the lower bound (using the sets \\(S_L\\) and \\(S_R\\)) and of the construction (explicit rectangles \\(H_i\\) and \\(V_j\\)).\n* All mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters.\n* No extraneous commentary or unverified claims remain."
